{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,300p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,300p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: project-management\ndescription: >\n  File-based task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration for AI agents.\n  Use when creating or maintaining boards, decomposing specs into Epic -> Story\n  -> Task/Bug work, tracking bugs, checking status/progress, or coordinating\n  execution through task-board. For board-driven execution, the active role of a\n  normal non-spawned session is ORCHESTRATOR (coordinator/supervisor): plan from\n  the critical path, create and refine board items, then delegate research,\n  implementation, testing, docs, and review to tracked background child runs\n  with explicit role, model, and reasoning selections; child-agent type follows\n  the current orchestrator runtime unless the human or task context explicitly\n  overrides it. Before launch,\n  inspect the project spawn policy: spawn.enabled can disable all child runs and\n  per-agent model_criterion=equal pins the executable model without model ranking;\n  ordered criteria use provider-local model ranks and require deliberate\n  one-line selection rationale plus content-bound acknowledgement after any\n  requested-to-resolved adjustment.\n  Spec-to-board decomposition is the Orchestrator's inline duty: never create a\n  meta-task or spawn solution-architect solely for board layout. Do not perform\n  task work inline unless the human explicitly asks for inline execution, except\n  for this mandatory board-layout duty.\n  Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи,\n  эпик, стори, таски, скил, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, агенты, критический\n  путь, декомпозиция, статус, прогресс, гол, голы, цель.\ntriggers:\n  - creating task board from spec\n  - breaking down spec into tasks\n  - project planning\n  - task management\n  - creating bugs\n  - bug tracking\n  - epic, story, task, board, task-board\n  - борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски\n  - спека, требования, декомпозиция, разбей на задачи\n  - что дальше, какие задачи, статус, прогресс, покажи борд\n  - spawn, orchestrator, coordinator, sub-agent, multi-agent\n  - goal, delivery goal, goal-scope, goal-bound pool\n  - спавн, оркестратор, координатор, субагент, агенты, критический путь\n  - гол, голы, цель, взять пул под гол\n  - work planning, what's next, status, progress, summary\n---\n\n# Project Management Skill\n\nFile-backed project management via `task-board` plus tracked child-process orchestration. Board state lives in git or a board-server; executable work is delegated through role-based spawned agents.\n\n## Active Role: Orchestrator\n\nYour active role is **Orchestrator**. In the `task-board` role registry, `orchestrator` and `coordinator` are the same supervisor identity; `coordinator` is the older implementation name.\n\nUnless the prompt explicitly gives you a spawned specialist role, `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, or the human explicitly asks for inline execution, do not act as a worker. Act as the parent Orchestrator.\n\nThe Orchestrator does board and orchestration work only:\n- create/refine epics, stories, tasks, bugs, AC, checklist DoD, dependencies, and resources\n- inspect `project_config()`, `summary()`, `plan(..., active=true)`, `agents()`, and `spawn` run state\n- choose the first unblocked critical-path work and spawn the right specialist roles\n- own each selected item through producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until a reviewer accepts it and the board reaches `done`\n- summarize child outcomes for routing/status and report status without delegating routine review back to the human\n\n### Inline Spec Decomposition\n\nSpec decomposition into board elements is the Orchestrator's own inline duty. Read the spec and directly create or refine its epics, stories, tasks/bugs, acceptance criteria, checklist DoD, and dependencies with `task-board`. This board-layout work is an explicit exception to the normal specialist-delegation rule.\n\nDo not create a meta-task whose sole purpose is to lay out a spec on the board. Do not spawn `solution-architect` merely to translate a settled spec into stories and tasks. A `solution-architect` spawn is valid only when the work contains a genuine architecture decision; after that decision, the Orchestrator still owns the resulting board layout inline.\n\nDelegation examples:\n\n- Invalid: create a `decompose-auth-spec` meta-task and spawn `solution-architect` solely to turn the settled auth spec into stories and tasks.\n- Invalid: spawn `solution-architect` to copy already-settled requirements, acceptance criteria, and dependencies into board elements.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to choose between event-driven and request/response integration across modules, then use the decision while laying out the board inline.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to define an ambiguous cross-module contract and ownership boundary before refining the affected stories and tasks inline.\n\nThis mandate avoids orchestration work that produces no executable scope: the wordstats probe spent two full `sol/max` sessions and 25 minutes on meta-delegated decomposition before the first real task existed.\n\nThe Orchestrator does not do specialist task work inline. The board-layout duty above is the explicit exception. Decision rule: if the next step would produce or validate a project artifact — research, code/config changes, tests, documentation, synthesis/decision docs, or a review verdict — prepare a clear board item and run a tracked background specialist:\n\n```bash\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Codex keeps Codex affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Claude keeps Claude affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def456 --role researcher --background --model claude-opus-4-8\n\n# Explicit human/task-context override: pass --agent and override affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi789 --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8\n```\n\nUse `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only at real dependency barriers. Parallelize independent work in the same phase; sequence producer -> reviewer -> dependent consumer work.\n\n## Delivery Ownership: Close the Loop\n\nOnce execution starts, the Orchestrator remains responsible for delivery:\n\n1. Monitor the producer until it reaches `to-review` with outcome evidence.\n2. Spawn and monitor a reviewer child. `to-review` is an immediate routing trigger, never a stopping point or a request for human review.\n3. If changes are requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, route to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the appropriate producer.\n4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.\n5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.\n6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.\n\nNever ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.\n\n## Project-State Monitoring\n\nUse the observability preset family for the Orchestrator's broad-to-narrow project-state loop. These are field presets on the existing query operations, not new operations:\n\n| Level | Query | Top-level projection |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Project | `summary() { observability-project }` | `observability` |\n| Epic | `list(type=epic) { observability-epic }` or `get(EPIC-...) { observability-epic }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Story | `get(STORY-...) { observability-story }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Task/Bug | `get(TASK-...) { observability-task }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n\nUse compact output for routine monitoring:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-abc123) { observability-story }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { observability-task }'\n```\n\n`observability-task` is also the leaf preset for Bugs. All four presets expose one stable nested shape:\n\n- `observability.scope` is `{type:\"project\", name:\"Project\"}` at project level and `{id, type, name}` for an element subtree.\n- `observability.summary` preserves the existing summary shape, scoped to the project or selected element plus its descendants: `byType` status counts, `active` element rows, and `blocked` rows with `blockedBy`, `derivedBlockedBy`, `isBlocked`, and `isAdvisoryBlocked`.\n- `observability.readiness.estimateKind` is the leaf or calculated estimate state: `notEstimated`, `estimated`, or `partiallyEstimated` as applicable.\n- `observability.readiness.coverage` always contains numeric `estimated`, `unestimated`, and `total`, string `ratio` such as `\"2/3\"`, and boolean `complete`.\n- `observability.readiness.rolledEstimate` is always the known sum shape `{kind:\"estimated\", scale:{kind:\"fibonacci\", number:N}}`, including when coverage is partial.\n- `observability.readiness.isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard blocking in the scope. `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` separately aggregate derived cross-container waiting. The ID arrays are sorted.\n\nPlain `summary()` keeps its legacy `{summary:{byType, active, blocked}}` response. Select `observability-project` when the readiness fields are needed.\n\n### Monitoring and routing loop\n\n1. **Project readiness:** start with `summary() { observability-project }`. Read `summary.active` for work that needs supervision or review routing, `summary.blocked` for blocker details, and `readiness` for aggregate estimate coverage and hard/advisory state. This is the fastest board-wide routing view.\n2. **Epic readiness:** list `observability-epic` rows to identify which delivery subtree owns a partial-coverage or blocker signal. Compare the top-level Epic `status` with its scoped `summary.active`; a quiet-looking container can still have active descendants, and a live container can still contain uncovered estimates.\n3. **Story readiness:** query the affected Story with `observability-story`. Story coverage is over its direct Task/Bug leaves; use the scoped active and blocked rows to decide which producer, reviewer, or blocker should move next.\n4. **Task/Bug readiness:** query the next leaf with `observability-task`. Coverage `1/1` plus no hard block means its estimate precondition is covered, but launch order still comes from dependencies and `plan(..., active=true)`. Coverage `0/1` or `estimateKind=notEstimated` means a new leaf needs an explicit estimate before entering `development`.\n\nWhen any project, Epic, or Story view reports `coverage.complete=false` or `estimateKind=partiallyEstimated`, do not call the rolled estimate a full-scope estimate. Narrow to incomplete Epics/Stories, then request the separate estimate projection:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(EPIC-260218-abc123) { estimate }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-def456) { estimate }'\n```\n\nThe partial `estimate` projection adds the exact `notEstimatedCount` and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds` to its known `rolledEstimate`. Set or route estimates for those concrete Task/Bug IDs before using the roll-up for capacity or scope decisions. Missing estimates are missing coverage, not zero-point work. Legacy leaves without a persisted Estimate section remain start-compatible, but still appear as unestimated in container projections.\n\nRead blocker signals independently:\n\n- `isBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is hard-blocked; inspect `blockedElementIds` and the matching `summary.blocked[*].blockedBy`, then advance those manual blockers before launching the dependent leaf.\n- `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is waiting because of derived cross-container ordering; inspect `advisoryBlockedElementIds` and `derivedBlockedBy`, schedule the source first, but do not treat advisory state as status enforcement.\n- Both flags may be true in the same scope. Never collapse a mixed signal into one generic blocked label.\n\n`coverage.complete` describes estimate coverage only; it is not a delivery-ready or accepted verdict. `rolledEstimate` sums all known scoped leaf estimates regardless of lifecycle status, so it is not remaining work. Combine observability with `plan(..., active=true)` for waves/critical path, `agents()` for assignment state, and `spawn status/events/watch/wait` for child-run health.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\nDo not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \"solution\" is mostly compensating hacks.\n\nThe Bluetooth permission/off-state failure pattern is the canonical warning sign: once the agent learned that iOS could not reveal powered-off Bluetooth state before permission without touching CoreBluetooth and potentially triggering the system prompt, it should have stopped, documented the constraint and options, and asked for a product decision. Adding more booleans, stubs, priority rules, and UI tests only made an invalid model look temporarily testable.\n\nPause or escalate only for either an evidence-backed external blocker that cannot be resolved autonomously or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only boundary—an impossible platform/API or product constraint, approval boundary, or unresolved product or architecture tradeoff—that cannot be derived from the task, AC, or existing decisions.\n\nWhen this happens:\n- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.\n- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.\n- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.\n- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.\n\nTests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\nRoutine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.\n\n---\n\n# Part 1: Task Management\n\n---\n\n## When to Use\n\n- New project with SPEC/requirements → break down into epics/stories/tasks\n- Need progress tracking in files\n- Working with AI agent on a project\n- Tracking bugs\n\n---\n\n## CLI Tool: `task-board`\n\nAll board operations go through the `task-board` CLI. No manual file editing.\n\n**Source repo:** your local `skill-project-management` checkout\n**Installed skill artifact:** `~/.agents/skills/project-management` (degitized copy for agent runtimes)\n**In PATH:** installed copy in `~/.local/bin/task-board`\n\nInstall/update the local binary before board setup:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nFor a safe reinstall that refreshes binaries, the installed skill copy, roles, and install metadata without touching the current project's `task-board.config.json`, use:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh --install-only\n```\n\n`--install-only` is also the supported verification gate for rollout drift. It checks the installed `task-board` binary with `project_config()` and local `models()` queries, requires the tracked background, explicit-selection, and child launch-composition contract fields plus current Codex registry to be present, verifies the installed `SKILL.md` matches this source artifact, and fails loud if PATH resolves a stale binary.\n\n`task-board self-update` relies on `~/.config/task-board/install.json`, which is written by `scripts/setup.sh`. Do not treat `~/.agents/skills/project-management` as the update source; it is the installed artifact copy and has git metadata scrubbed.\n\n`scripts/setup.sh` is the single supported install/update entrypoint for `task-board`, `task-board-tui`, and the installed skill artifact. `make install-*` targets are binary build helpers and do not replace setup.\n\nWhen you bootstrap board-driven work inside a real code repository, prefer a single source-checkout setup command that also bootstraps the repo-local agent runtime through `relux-agents-infra`:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\n```\n\n`--bootstrap-local-agents` runs `agents-infra setup local /path/to/project` after writing the project config. Install the global `agents-infra` launcher once from the `relux-agents-infra` source repo via `./setup.sh`, then use this shortcut for project repos.\n\nThat local setup makes project `.agents/` the installed runtime copy and refreshes `.claude/` + `.codex/` as thin shims into it. Do this right after board/config setup so spawned agents see the repo-local instructions, skills, and templates instead of drifting onto only global state.\n\n### Quick Reference\n\nAll reads use `q` (query DSL), all writes use `m` (mutate DSL). Compact format (`--format compact`) saves tokens.\n\n```bash\n# Queries (reads)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations, mutations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # effective runtime + spawn.enabled and per-agent model criteria; run before spawning/audits\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # board overview (counts + active/blocked)\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc123) { full }'                 # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # filtered list\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # blocked tasks\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'  # stories in epic\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'                             # local spawn model/effort registry (also in remote board mode)\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=qwen)'                              # Qwen model/effort/context/Token Plan registry\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc123, mode=children)'          # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc123, STORY-260218-def456], mode=children)'  # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\ntask-board q --format compact 'count(type=task, status=done)'                    # count elements\n\n# Mutations (writes)\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\"...\")'                        # create epic\ntask-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123, name=audio-capture)'            # create story\ntask-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=impl, description=\"...\")'  # create task\ntask-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-abc123, title=\"new title\", description=\"...\")'        # update README fields\ntask-board m 'rename(TASK-260218-abc123, name=better-task-name)'                            # update overview name/slug without changing ID\ntask-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc123, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'  # estimate Task/Bug work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'                           # change status\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc123, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'      # acknowledge an enforced version-control done transition\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus)'                                # assign agent\ntask-board m 'unassign(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                                 # remove assignment\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                      # add dependency\n---\nname: go-testing-tools\ndescription: >\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\ntriggers:\n  - go test\n  - bubbletea test\n  - tuitestkit\n  - go test helpers\n  - reducer test\n  - snapshot test go\n  - golden file go\n  - тесты го\n  - го тест хелперы\n  - тесты баблти\n  - тест утилиты го\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n---\n\n# Go Testing Tools\n\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **Go 1.21+**\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n\nInstall the library in your project:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n\n```go\npackage myscreen_test\n\nimport (\n    \"testing\"\n\n    \"github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\"\n)\n\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n\n    // Press down twice\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"), tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"))\n\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, \"third item\")\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, \"ERROR\")\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing Pyramid\n\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 > Level 2 > Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n\n---\n\n## Library API Reference\n\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n\n```go\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n// Supports: \"enter\", \"tab\", \"esc\", \"space\", \"backspace\", \"up\", \"down\",\n// \"left\", \"right\", \"home\", \"end\", \"pgup\", \"pgdown\", \"delete\", \"insert\",\n// \"f1\"-\"f20\", \"ctrl+c\", \"ctrl+a\"...\"ctrl+z\", \"alt+h\", \"alt+enter\",\n// single runes: \"a\", \"/\", \"1\", etc.\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n```\n\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n\n```go\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n// Init() is NOT called.\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n```\n\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n\n```go\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"r\"))\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n```\n\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n\n```go\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n// S = state type, A = action type.\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Action  A\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name   string\n    Action A\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n```\n\n**Invariant checking:**\n\n```go\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\n    Name  string\n    Check func(s S) error\n}\n\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```go\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n        Name: \"cursor in bounds\",\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n            if s.Cursor < 0 || s.Cursor >= len(s.Items) {\n                return fmt.Errorf(\"cursor %d out of [0, %d)\", s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n            }\n            return nil\n        },\n    },\n)\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n```\n\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n\n```go\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\ntype MockCall struct {\n    Method string\n    Args   []any\n}\n\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n\n// Assertion helpers\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n```\n\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n\n```go\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n}\n\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n    return &TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(\"TreeJSON\")\n    return m.Responses.Get(\"TreeJSON\")\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(\"Execute\", cmd, args)\n    return m.Responses.Get(\"Execute:\" + cmd)\n}\n```\n\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n\n```go\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
