{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n -C 2 \"legacy.*compar|rc.4.*compar|schema-7 prose|accepted.*compar|baseline\" /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-17sclp/TASK-260728-17sclp_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-_RUN-260727-26b743.log | tail -n 120'","aggregated_output":"5-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ve moved the task into development. I’m using the project-management skill for the required board lifecycle and the Go testing skill for curator-spec’s schema fixtures and validation loop; I’ll first map the repository conventions and current schema/version generators before changing files.\"}}\n6-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && sed -n '261,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && for f in /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_PLATFORM.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STRUCTURE.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_ATTACHMENTS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILL_TRIGGERS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DIAGRAMS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_WORKFLOW.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DOCS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STYLE.md; do sed -n '1,260p' \\\\\\\"\\\"'$f\\\"; done'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && sed -n '261,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && for f in /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_PLATFORM.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STRUCTURE.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_ATTACHMENTS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILL_TRIGGERS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DIAGRAMS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_WORKFLOW.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DOCS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STYLE.md; do sed -n '1,260p' \\\\\\\"\\\"'$f\\\"; done'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: project-management\\ndescription: >\\n  File-based task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration for AI agents.\\n  Use when creating or maintaining boards, decomposing specs into Epic -> Story\\n  -> Task/Bug work, tracking bugs, checking status/progress, or coordinating\\n  execution through task-board. For board-driven execution, the active role of a\\n  normal non-spawned session is ORCHESTRATOR (coordinator/supervisor): plan from\\n  the critical path, create and refine board items, then delegate research,\\n  implementation, testing, docs, and review to tracked background child runs\\n  with explicit role, model, and reasoning selections; child-agent type follows\\n  the current orchestrator runtime unless the human or task context explicitly\\n  overrides it. Before launch,\\n  inspect the project spawn policy: spawn.enabled can disable all child runs and\\n  per-agent model_criterion=equal pins the executable model without model ranking;\\n  ordered criteria use provider-local model ranks and require deliberate\\n  one-line selection rationale plus content-bound acknowledgement after any\\n  requested-to-resolved adjustment.\\n  Spec-to-board decomposition is the Orchestrator's inline duty: never create a\\n  meta-task or spawn solution-architect solely for board layout. Do not perform\\n  task work inline unless the human explicitly asks for inline execution, except\\n  for this mandatory board-layout duty.\\n  Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи,\\n  эпик, стори, таски, скил, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, агенты, критический\\n  путь, декомпозиция, статус, прогресс, гол, голы, цель.\\ntriggers:\\n  - creating task board from spec\\n  - breaking down spec into tasks\\n  - project planning\\n  - task management\\n  - creating bugs\\n  - bug tracking\\n  - epic, story, task, board, task-board\\n  - борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски\\n  - спека, требования, декомпозиция, разбей на задачи\\n  - что дальше, какие задачи, статус, прогресс, покажи борд\\n  - spawn, orchestrator, coordinator, sub-agent, multi-agent\\n  - goal, delivery goal, goal-scope, goal-bound pool\\n  - спавн, оркестратор, координатор, субагент, агенты, критический путь\\n  - гол, голы, цель, взять пул под гол\\n  - work planning, what's next, status, progress, summary\\n---\\n\\n# Project Management Skill\\n\\nFile-backed project management via `task-board` plus tracked child-process orchestration. Board state lives in git or a board-server; executable work is delegated through role-based spawned agents.\\n\\n## Active Role: Orchestrator\\n\\nYour active role is **Orchestrator**. In the `task-board` role registry, `orchestrator` and `coordinator` are the same supervisor identity; `coordinator` is the older implementation name.\\n\\nUnless the prompt explicitly gives you a spawned specialist role, `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, or the human explicitly asks for inline execution, do not act as a worker. Act as the parent Orchestrator.\\n\\nThe Orchestrator does board and orchestration work only:\\n- create/refine epics, stories, tasks, bugs, AC, checklist DoD, dependencies, and resources\\n- inspect `project_config()`, `summary()`, `plan(..., active=true)`, `agents()`, and `spawn` run state\\n- choose the first unblocked critical-path work and spawn the right specialist roles\\n- own each selected item through producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until a reviewer accepts it and the board reaches `done`\\n- summarize child outcomes for routing/status and report status without delegating routine review back to the human\\n\\n### Inline Spec Decomposition\\n\\nSpec decomposition into board elements is the Orchestrator's own inline duty. Read the spec and directly create or refine its epics, stories, tasks/bugs, acceptance criteria, checklist DoD, and dependencies with `task-board`. This board-layout work is an explicit exception to the normal specialist-delegation rule.\\n\\nDo not create a meta-task whose sole purpose is to lay out a spec on the board. Do not spawn `solution-architect` merely to translate a settled spec into stories and tasks. A `solution-architect` spawn is valid only when the work contains a genuine architecture decision; after that decision, the Orchestrator still owns the resulting board layout inline.\\n\\nDelegation examples:\\n\\n- Invalid: create a `decompose-auth-spec` meta-task and spawn `solution-architect` solely to turn the settled auth spec into stories and tasks.\\n- Invalid: spawn `solution-architect` to copy already-settled requirements, acceptance criteria, and dependencies into board elements.\\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to choose between event-driven and request/response integration across modules, then use the decision while laying out the board inline.\\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to define an ambiguous cross-module contract and ownership boundary before refining the affected stories and tasks inline.\\n\\nThis mandate avoids orchestration work that produces no executable scope: the wordstats probe spent two full `sol/max` sessions and 25 minutes on meta-delegated decomposition before the first real task existed.\\n\\nThe Orchestrator does not do specialist task work inline. The board-layout duty above is the explicit exception. Decision rule: if the next step would produce or validate a project artifact — research, code/config changes, tests, documentation, synthesis/decision docs, or a review verdict — prepare a clear board item and run a tracked background specialist:\\n\\n```bash\\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Codex keeps Codex affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Claude keeps Claude affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def456 --role researcher --background --model claude-opus-4-8\\n\\n# Explicit human/task-context override: pass --agent and override affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi789 --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8\\n```\\n\\nUse `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only at real dependency barriers. Parallelize independent work in the same phase; sequence producer -> reviewer -> dependent consumer work.\\n\\n## Delivery Ownership: Close the Loop\\n\\nOnce execution starts, the Orchestrator remains responsible for delivery:\\n\\n1. Monitor the producer until it reaches `to-review` with outcome evidence.\\n2. Spawn and monitor a reviewer child. `to-review` is an immediate routing trigger, never a stopping point or a request for human review.\\n3. If changes are requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, route to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the appropriate producer.\\n4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.\\n5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.\\n6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.\\n\\nNever ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.\\n\\n## Project-State Monitoring\\n\\nUse the observability preset family for the Orchestrator's broad-to-narrow project-state loop. These are field presets on the existing query operations, not new operations:\\n\\n| Level | Query | Top-level projection |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Project | `summary() { observability-project }` | `observability` |\\n| Epic | `list(type=epic) { observability-epic }` or `get(EPIC-...) { observability-epic }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n| Story | `get(STORY-...) { observability-story }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n| Task/Bug | `get(TASK-...) { observability-task }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n\\nUse compact output for routine monitoring:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-abc123) { observability-story }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { observability-task }'\\n```\\n\\n`observability-task` is also the leaf preset for Bugs. All four presets expose one stable nested shape:\\n\\n- `observability.scope` is `{type:\\\"project\\\", name:\\\"Project\\\"}` at project level and `{id, type, name}` for an element subtree.\\n- `observability.summary` preserves the existing summary shape, scoped to the project or selected element plus its descendants: `byType` status counts, `active` element rows, and `blocked` rows with `blockedBy`, `derivedBlockedBy`, `isBlocked`, and `isAdvisoryBlocked`.\\n- `observability.readiness.estimateKind` is the leaf or calculated estimate state: `notEstimated`, `estimated`, or `partiallyEstimated` as applicable.\\n- `observability.readiness.coverage` always contains numeric `estimated`, `unestimated`, and `total`, string `ratio` such as `\\\"2/3\\\"`, and boolean `complete`.\\n- `observability.readiness.rolledEstimate` is always the known sum shape `{kind:\\\"estimated\\\", scale:{kind:\\\"fibonacci\\\", number:N}}`, including when coverage is partial.\\n- `observability.readiness.isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard blocking in the scope. `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` separately aggregate derived cross-container waiting. The ID arrays are sorted.\\n\\nPlain `summary()` keeps its legacy `{summary:{byType, active, blocked}}` response. Select `observability-project` when the readiness fields are needed.\\n\\n### Monitoring and routing loop\\n\\n1. **Project readiness:** start with `summary() { observability-project }`. Read `summary.active` for work that needs supervision or review routing, `summary.blocked` for blocker details, and `readiness` for aggregate estimate coverage and hard/advisory state. This is the fastest board-wide routing view.\\n2. **Epic readiness:** list `observability-epic` rows to identify which delivery subtree owns a partial-coverage or blocker signal. Compare the top-level Epic `status` with its scoped `summary.active`; a quiet-looking container can still have active descendants, and a live container can still contain uncovered estimates.\\n3. **Story readiness:** query the affected Story with `observability-story`. Story coverage is over its direct Task/Bug leaves; use the scoped active and blocked rows to decide which producer, reviewer, or blocker should move next.\\n4. **Task/Bug readiness:** query the next leaf with `observability-task`. Coverage `1/1` plus no hard block means its estimate precondition is covered, but launch order still comes from dependencies and `plan(..., active=true)`. Coverage `0/1` or `estimateKind=notEstimated` means a new leaf needs an explicit estimate before entering `development`.\\n\\nWhen any project, Epic, or Story view reports `coverage.complete=false` or `estimateKind=partiallyEstimated`, do not call the rolled estimate a full-scope estimate. Narrow to incomplete Epics/Stories, then request the separate estimate projection:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(EPIC-260218-abc123) { estimate }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-def456) { estimate }'\\n```\\n\\nThe partial `estimate` projection adds the exact `notEstimatedCount` and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds` to its known `rolledEstimate`. Set or route estimates for those concrete Task/Bug IDs before using the roll-up for capacity or scope decisions. Missing estimates are missing coverage, not zero-point work. Legacy leaves without a persisted Estimate section remain start-compatible, but still appear as unestimated in container projections.\\n\\nRead blocker signals independently:\\n\\n- `isBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is hard-blocked; inspect `blockedElementIds` and the matching `summary.blocked[*].blockedBy`, then advance those manual blockers before launching the dependent leaf.\\n- `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is waiting because of derived cross-container ordering; inspect `advisoryBlockedElementIds` and `derivedBlockedBy`, schedule the source first, but do not treat advisory state as status enforcement.\\n- Both flags may be true in the same scope. Never collapse a mixed signal into one generic blocked label.\\n\\n`coverage.complete` describes estimate coverage only; it is not a delivery-ready or accepted verdict. `rolledEstimate` sums all known scoped leaf estimates regardless of lifecycle status, so it is not remaining work. Combine observability with `plan(..., active=true)` for waves/critical path, `agents()` for assignment state, and `spawn status/events/watch/wait` for child-run health.\\n\\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\\n\\nDo not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \\\"solution\\\" is mostly compensating hacks.\\n\\nThe Bluetooth permission/off-state failure pattern is the canonical warning sign: once the agent learned that iOS could not reveal powered-off Bluetooth state before permission without touching CoreBluetooth and potentially triggering the system prompt, it should have stopped, documented the constraint and options, and asked for a product decision. Adding more booleans, stubs, priority rules, and UI tests only made an invalid model look temporarily testable.\\n\\nPause or escalate only for either an evidence-backed external blocker that cannot be resolved autonomously or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only boundary—an impossible platform/API or product constraint, approval boundary, or unresolved product or architecture tradeoff—that cannot be derived from the task, AC, or existing decisions.\\n\\nWhen this happens:\\n- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.\\n- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.\\n- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.\\n- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.\\n\\nTests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\\nRoutine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Part 1: Task Management\\n\\n---\\n\\n## When to Use\\n\\n- New project with SPEC/requirements → break down into epics/stories/tasks\\n- Need progress tracking in files\\n- Working with AI agent on a project\\n- Tracking bugs\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CLI Tool: `task-board`\\n\\nAll board operations go through the `task-board` CLI. No manual file editing.\\n\\n**Source repo:** your local `skill-project-management` checkout\\n**Installed skill artifact:** `~/.agents/skills/project-management` (degitized copy for agent runtimes)\\n**In PATH:** installed copy in `~/.local/bin/task-board`\\n\\nInstall/update the local binary before board setup:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nFor a safe reinstall that refreshes binaries, the installed skill copy, roles, and install metadata without touching the current project's `task-board.config.json`, use:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/setup.sh --install-only\\n```\\n\\n`--install-only` is also the supported verification gate for rollout drift. It checks the installed `task-board` binary with `project_config()` and local `models()` queries, requires the tracked background, explicit-selection, and child launch-composition contract fields plus current Codex registry to be present, verifies the installed `SKILL.md` matches this source artifact, and fails loud if PATH resolves a stale binary.\\n\\n`task-board self-update` relies on `~/.config/task-board/install.json`, which is written by `scripts/setup.sh`. Do not treat `~/.agents/skills/project-management` as the update source; it is the installed artifact copy and has git metadata scrubbed.\\n\\n`scripts/setup.sh` is the single supported install/update entrypoint for `task-board`, `task-board-tui`, and the installed skill artifact. `make install-*` targets are binary build helpers and do not replace setup.\\n\\nWhen you bootstrap board-driven work inside a real code repository, prefer a single source-checkout setup command that also bootstraps the repo-local agent runtime through `relux-agents-infra`:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\\n```\\n\\n`--bootstrap-local-agents` runs `agents-infra setup local /path/to/project` after writing the project config. Install the global `agents-infra` launcher once from the `relux-agents-infra` source repo via `./setup.sh`, then use this shortcut for project repos.\\n\\nThat local setup makes project `.agents/` the installed runtime copy and refreshes `.claude/` + `.codex/` as thin shims into it. Do this right after board/config setup so spawned agents see the repo-local instructions, skills, and templates instead of drifting onto only global state.\\n\\n### Quick Reference\\n\\nAll reads use `q` (query DSL), all writes use `m` (mutate DSL). Compact format (`--format compact`) saves tokens.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Queries (reads)\\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations, mutations\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # effective runtime + spawn.enabled and per-agent model criteria; run before spawning/audits\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # board overview (counts + active/blocked)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc123) { full }'                 # full element details\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # filtered list\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # blocked tasks\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'  # stories in epic\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'                             # local spawn model/effort registry (also in remote board mode)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=qwen)'                              # Qwen model/effort/context/Token Plan registry\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc123, mode=children)'          # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc123, STORY-260218-def456], mode=children)'  # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'count(type=task, status=done)'                    # count elements\\n\\n# Mutations (writes)\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\\\"...\\\")'                        # create epic\\ntask-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123, name=audio-capture)'            # create story\\ntask-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=impl, description=\\\"...\\\")'  # create task\\ntask-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-abc123, title=\\\"new title\\\", description=\\\"...\\\")'        # update README fields\\ntask-board m 'rename(TASK-260218-abc123, name=better-task-name)'                            # update overview name/slug without changing ID\\ntask-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc123, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'  # estimate Task/Bug work\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'                           # change status\\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc123, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'      # acknowledge an enforced version-control done transition\\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus)'                                # assign agent\\ntask-board m 'unassign(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                                 # remove assignment\\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                      # add dependency\\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                    # remove dependency\\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc123, item=1)'                                       # check checklist item\\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Write tests\\\")'                   # add checklist item\\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Progress update\\\")'                        # append notes\\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Full replacement\\\", set=true)'             # replace notes\\ntask-board m 'set_parent(TASK-260218-abc123, parent=STORY-260218-def456)'                   # move to new parent\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'  # create resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"revised\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design v2\\\")'  # replace resource\\ntask-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # preview destructive op\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # execute destructive op\\n\\n# Batching (semicolons)\\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus); set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'\\n\\n# Spawn tracked background runs (--role, --background, and --model are REQUIRED; Codex/Qwen also require --reasoning-effort)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8 --budget 5.0\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark --reasoning-effort xhigh\\ntask-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123                                            # inspect tracked run state\\ntask-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123                                              # block on tracked run state after RUN exists\\ntask-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123                                            # replay durable run events (supports --cursor/--follow)\\ntask-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123                                             # follow durable run-event notifications\\n\\n# Auth (always local, never sent to server)\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_4lc6...                                        # store token in Keychain and select active user outside repo config\\ntask-board login admin tb_UJTW... --remote https://board.example.com          # explicit remote bootstrap\\ntask-board logout                                                              # remove selected user's token\\ntask-board whoami                                                              # show remote URL + active user + token status\\ntask-board switch-user admin                                                   # switch active user without touching versioned config\\ntask-board q 'whoami()'                                                        # whoami via query DSL\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                # mode, board source, and effective spawn policy\\ntask-board m 'login(user=sampleuser, token=tb_xxx)'                           # login via mutation DSL\\ntask-board m 'switch_user(user=admin)'                                         # switch via mutation DSL\\n\\n# CLI utilities\\ntask-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"                            # full-text search across board files (local only)\\ntask-board validate                                        # check board structure (local only)\\ntask-board repair-links                                    # list unsupported stored container links (local, read-only)\\ntask-board repair-links STORY-260218-abc123 --confirm      # remove selected suspicious links\\ntask-board tui                                             # interactive terminal dashboard\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Task Board Structure\\n\\n### Hierarchy\\n\\n```\\nEpic → Story → Task/Bug\\n```\\n\\n- **Epic** = large feature / module\\n- **Story** = specific functionality within epic\\n- **Task** = atomic unit of work\\n- **Bug** = defect tracked within a story\\n\\n### Naming Convention\\n\\n**Distributed IDs** — each element gets a unique ID based on date + random hash.\\n\\n```\\nEPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\\n  STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\\n    TASK-260203-g7h8i9_audiorecorder-interface/\\n    TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\\n    BUG-260203-m3n4o5_some-bug/\\n```\\n\\nFormat: `{TYPE}-{YYMMDD}-{hash}_{kebab-case-name}`\\n- `EPIC-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `STORY-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `TASK-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `BUG-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n\\nWhere:\\n- `YYMMDD` — creation date (year, month, day)\\n- `xxxxxx` — 6-character base36 hash (collision-resistant)\\n\\nIDs are globally unique and work in parallel environments (no counter conflicts).\\n\\n### Directory Layout\\n\\n```\\n.task-board/\\n├── .resources/                   # Flat resource storage (managed by CLI)\\n│   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/       # Resources keyed by element ID\\n│   │   ├── design.md             # Precondition: input for the task\\n│   │   └── results.md            # Outcome: work product\\n│   └── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\\n│       └── research.md\\n├── EPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\\n│   ├── README.md                 # Epic description, scope, AC\\n│   ├── progress.md               # Status, blockedBy, checklist, notes, resource refs\\n│   ├── STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\\n│   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   ├── progress.md\\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_interface/\\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\\n│   │   └── BUG-260203-m3n4o5_crash-on-start/\\n│   │       ├── README.md\\n│   │       └── progress.md\\n│   └── STORY-260203-p6q7r8_amplitude/\\n│       └── ...\\n└── EPIC-260203-s9t0u1_storage/\\n    └── ...\\n```\\n\\nNo `system.md` needed — IDs are self-contained and don't require global counters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Statuses\\n\\n9 statuses: `backlog` → `analysis` → `to-dev` → `development` → `to-review` → `reviewing` → `done`. Plus `closed` and `blocked`.\\n\\nBefore a newly created Task/Bug enters `development`, set its estimate with\\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The start\\ntransition returns a typed estimate-required error while the explicit value is\\n`notEstimated`. Legacy Task/Bug files that have no Estimate section are\\ngrandfathered and remain startable.\\n\\n`done` is accepted leaf work or computed container completion; `closed` is an\\nexplicit administrative disposition. A story or epic may remain `closed` only\\nwhile every direct and transitive descendant is `done` or `closed`.\\n\\nFor full status flow, auto-promotion/reopen, blocked vs is_blocked — see [`references/statuses.md`](references/statuses.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## File Formats\\n\\nEach element directory contains two files:\\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\\n\\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\\n\\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Resources & Bugs\\n\\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create resource\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings\\\")'\\n\\n# Replace existing resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"revised findings\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings v2\\\")'\\n\\n# Delete resource\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\\n```\\n\\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\\n\\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\\n\\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\\n\\n#### Spec\\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\\n\\n#### Plan on the Board\\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\\n\\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\\n\\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\\n\\nKey points:\\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\\n\\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\\n\\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\\n\\nKey points:\\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\\n\\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\\n\\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\\n\\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\", ac=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\\n\\n### Project Start\\n\\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\\n\\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\\n\\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\\n\\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\\n\\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\\n\\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\\n\\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\\n\\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\\n---\\nname: go-testing-tools\\ndescription: >\\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\\ntriggers:\\n  - go test\\n  - bubbletea test\\n  - tuitestkit\\n  - go test helpers\\n  - reducer test\\n  - snapshot test go\\n  - golden file go\\n  - тесты го\\n  - го тест хелперы\\n  - тесты баблти\\n  - тест утилиты го\\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\\n---\\n\\n# Go Testing Tools\\n\\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\\n\\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Prerequisites\\n\\n- **Go 1.21+**\\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\\n\\nInstall the library in your project:\\n\\n```bash\\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\\n\\n```go\\npackage myscreen_test\\n\\nimport (\\n    \\\"testing\\\"\\n\\n    \\\"github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\\\"\\n)\\n\\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\\n\\n    // Press down twice\\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(\\\"down\\\"), tuitestkit.Key(\\\"down\\\"))\\n\\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, \\\"third item\\\")\\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, \\\"ERROR\\\")\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Testing Pyramid\\n\\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\\n\\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\\n\\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 > Level 2 > Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\\n\\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Library API Reference\\n\\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\\n\\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\\n\\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\\n\\n```go\\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\\n// Supports: \\\"enter\\\", \\\"tab\\\", \\\"esc\\\", \\\"space\\\", \\\"backspace\\\", \\\"up\\\", \\\"down\\\",\\n// \\\"left\\\", \\\"right\\\", \\\"home\\\", \\\"end\\\", \\\"pgup\\\", \\\"pgdown\\\", \\\"delete\\\", \\\"insert\\\",\\n// \\\"f1\\\"-\\\"f20\\\", \\\"ctrl+c\\\", \\\"ctrl+a\\\"...\\\"ctrl+z\\\", \\\"alt+h\\\", \\\"alt+enter\\\",\\n// single runes: \\\"a\\\", \\\"/\\\", \\\"1\\\", etc.\\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\\n\\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\\n\\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\\n\\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\\n\\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\\n\\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\\n\\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\\n```\\n\\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\\n\\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\\n\\n```go\\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\\n// Init() is NOT called.\\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\\n\\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\\n\\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\\n```\\n\\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\\n\\n```go\\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(\\\"r\\\"))\\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\\n```\\n\\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\\n\\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\\n\\n```go\\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\\n// S = state type, A = action type.\\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\\n    Name    string\\n    Initial S\\n    Action  A\\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\\n}\\n\\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\\n    Name   string\\n    Action A\\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\\n}\\n\\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\\n    Name    string\\n    Initial S\\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\\n}\\n\\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\\n\\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\\n```\\n\\n**Invariant checking:**\\n\\n```go\\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\\n    Name  string\\n    Check func(s S) error\\n}\\n\\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\\n\\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\\n\\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\\n\\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\\n```\\n\\n**Example:**\\n\\n```go\\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\\n        Name: \\\"cursor in bounds\\\",\\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\\n            if s.Cursor < 0 || s.Cursor >= len(s.Items) {\\n                return fmt.Errorf(\\\"cursor %d out of [0, %d)\\\", s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\\n            }\\n            return nil\\n        },\\n    },\\n)\\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\\n```\\n\\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\\n\\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\\n\\n```go\\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\\ntype MockCall struct {\\n    Method string\\n    Args   []any\\n}\\n\\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\\n\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\\n\\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\\n\\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\\n\\n// Assertion helpers\\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\\n```\\n\\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\\n\\n```go\\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\\n}\\n\\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\\n    return &TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\\n}\\n\\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\\n    m.Record(\\\"TreeJSON\\\")\\n    return m.Responses.Get(\\\"TreeJSON\\\")\\n}\\n\\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\\n    m.Record(\\\"Execute\\\", cmd, args)\\n    return m.Responses.Get(\\\"Execute:\\\" + cmd)\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\\n\\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\\n\\n```go\\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\\n\\n// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\\n\\nfunc ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\\nfunc ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\\nfunc ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\\nfunc ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\\nfunc ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\\nfunc ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\\n\\n// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\\n\\nfunc ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\\nfunc NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\\nfunc LinesFromStr(view string) []string\\nfunc MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\\n```\\n\\n### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\\n\\nGolden file comparison for visual regression testing.\\n\\n```go\\n// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\\n// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\\nvar UpdateSnapshots bool\\n\\n// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\\nfunc SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\\n\\n// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\\nfunc SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\\n\\n// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\\nfunc SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\\n\\n// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\\nfunc SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\\n```\\n\\nGolden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/<name>.golden` relative to the test file.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create or update golden files\\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\\n\\n# Update for a single package\\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\\n```\\n\\nMismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Closed-Loop Workflow\\n\\nThe agent development cycle for writing tests:\\n\\n```\\n1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\\n2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\\n3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\\n4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\\n5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\\n6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\\n7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\\n8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\\n9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\\n```\\n\\n### Step Details\\n\\n**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\\n- What state does it manage?\\n- What messages does it handle in Update?\\n- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\\n- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\\n- What does View() render?\\n\\n**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\\n- Pure state logic -> Level 1 (reducer tests)\\n- Key bindings, view rendering -> Level 2 (component tests)\\n- Data loading, command execution -> Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\\n- Visual appearance -> Level 4 (snapshot tests)\\n\\n**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\\n1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\\n2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\\n3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\\n4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\\n\\n**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\\n- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\\n- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\\n- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\\n- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\\n\\n**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\\n\\n**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\\n\\n**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\\n- Type assertion panic -> `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\\n- View assertion failure -> check ANSI stripping, check exact text\\n- Snapshot mismatch -> run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\\n- Mock assertion failure -> check response key matches (`\\\"Execute:list\\\"` not `\\\"Execute\\\"`)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Templates\\n\\nScaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\\n\\n| Template | Purpose |\\n|----------|---------|\\n| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\\n| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\\n| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\\n| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\\n| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\\n| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\\n\\nCopy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## References\\n\\nIn-depth documentation in `references/`:\\n\\n| Document | Content |\\n|----------|---------|\\n| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\\n| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\\n| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Setup\\n\\nRun the setup script to create symlinks:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nThis links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\\n\\nTo remove:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/deinit.sh\\n```\\n# Platform\\n\\n* Before building/testing, **inspect the project** to determine the intended target platform(s).\\n* If multiple platforms are supported, prefer **iOS** when available.\\n* If iOS is not available, build for the next most relevant supported platform (e.g. **macOS**, etc.).\\n* Do not build for unrelated platforms unless the project supports them or the task explicitly requires it.\\n# Project Structure & Environment\\n\\n## Shell & Environment\\n\\n* Shell: **zsh**\\n* Shell framework: **oh-my-zsh**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Project Structure\\n\\n* `.temp/` — temporary utilities, logs, debugging artifacts (**gitignored**).\\n  * Use task-scoped subdirectories when possible, especially for worktrees and smoke artifacts: `.temp/<TASK-ID>/...`\\n  * Temporary `git worktree` checkouts should live under `.temp/`, not beside the main repo checkout.\\n* `.scripts/` — reusable scripts and utilities for the project.\\n* `diagrams/` (project root) — all diagrams live here.\\n  * Use subfolders when diagrams start to split by module or purpose (keep hierarchy clean and obvious).\\n# Attachments\\n\\nUse this contract when the runtime materializes user-provided files for the agent.\\n\\n## Contract\\n\\n- Manifest file name: `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n- Environment variable: `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\\n- Default project-local fallback: `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n\\nThe manifest may be either:\\n\\n- a JSON array of attachment objects\\n- or an object with top-level key `attachments`\\n\\nEach attachment object should contain:\\n\\n- `id` — stable attachment identifier\\n- `name` — original file name\\n- `mime_type` — media type such as `image/png`\\n- `size_bytes` — byte size\\n- `sha256` — optional content hash\\n- `local_path` — absolute local path to the materialized file\\n\\nOptional fields:\\n\\n- `source`\\n- `created_at`\\n- `metadata`\\n\\n## Agent behavior\\n\\n- Treat `local_path` as the source of truth for file access.\\n- Treat incoming attachments as read-only inputs.\\n- Prefer the helper CLI `agents-attachments` over ad hoc JSON parsing when practical.\\n- If a tool needs a file path, pass `local_path` directly.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n```bash\\nagents-attachments materialize\\nagents-attachments list\\nagents-attachments path screenshot.png\\nmkdir -p .temp/attachments\\ncp \\\"$(agents-attachments path screenshot.png)\\\" .temp/attachments/\\n```\\n\\n## Codex bootstrap path\\n\\nIf the runtime did not pre-materialize files but Codex rollout history is available, the helper can bootstrap a local manifest from the current thread:\\n\\n```bash\\nagents-attachments materialize\\n```\\n\\nBehavior:\\n\\n- uses `CODEX_THREAD_ID` by default\\n- locates the matching `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl`\\n- extracts `input_image` payloads from user turns\\n- writes files under `.temp/agents-attachments/`\\n- writes `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n\\nOverrides:\\n\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --thread-id ...`\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --session /abs/path/to/rollout.jsonl`\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --out-dir .temp/custom-attachments`\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --manifest .temp/custom-manifest.json`\\n\\n## Spawned agents\\n\\nWhen a parent process spawns a child agent, it should:\\n\\n- propagate `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\\n- allow access to the directory that contains the materialized files\\n- keep child agents on the same manifest unless there is an explicit reason to narrow scope\\n\\n## Runtime boundary\\n\\nThis repo defines the contract and helper tooling. It does not itself ingest chat attachments.\\n\\nThe runtime/launcher is responsible for:\\n\\n- extracting files from the host conversation layer\\n- materializing them on disk\\n- writing `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n- exporting `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\\n# Allowed Tools\\n\\nAgent may use these tools freely.\\n\\n## Build / Apple tooling\\n\\n* `xcodebuild` (CLI builds/tests)\\n* `swift` (toolchain / SwiftPM)\\n* `simctl` (simulator management)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Scripting / Automation\\n\\n### git\\n\\n* Inspect diffs, config, repo state.\\n* Agent may check git config to detect default branch and repo location.\\n* **Never commit or stage files automatically.**\\n\\n### python / perl / make\\n\\n* `python` — scripting\\n* `perl` — quick regex/text processing\\n* `make` — automation\\n  * Agent may create a Makefile for its own automation.\\n  * If a Makefile already exists, extend it (do not replace).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Installing additional CLI tools\\n\\n* If the agent identifies a **strong, justified need** for additional CLI tools, it may:\\n  * ask to install them, **or**\\n  * attempt to install them directly **if permissions allow**.\\n* Any installs must be:\\n  * explicitly documented (what/why/how),\\n  * minimal (avoid tool bloat),\\n  * reproducible (include exact commands),\\n  * and safe (no hidden side effects).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tool Readiness Check (mandatory)\\n\\n* Before using any tool for real project work, the agent must verify it can run and produce expected output.\\n* Verification outputs / scratch artifacts must be stored in `.temp/` (since it is not versioned).\\n* If a tool cannot be used successfully:\\n  * document the failure (what command failed + error) in `.temp/` logs and/or `.temp/tasks.md`;\\n  * do not proceed with workflows that assume the tool works.\\n# Skills & Plugins\\n\\n## Using Skills (IMPORTANT)\\n\\n**Before performing any technical task, check if a relevant skill exists.**\\n\\n* Search `agents/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.codex/skills/` for applicable skills.\\n* If a skill covers the task — **read and follow it**, don't improvise.\\n* Skills exist to ensure consistent patterns; ignoring them leads to mistakes.\\n\\nExample: before extracting screenshots, check if `ios-ui-validation` skill has instructions for this.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Skill Locations\\n\\n| Tool | Global | Project-local |\\n|------|--------|---------------|\\n| Claude Code | `~/.claude/skills/` | `.claude/skills/` |\\n| Codex CLI | `~/.codex/skills/` | `.codex/skills/` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Editing Shared Skills & Instructions (CRITICAL)\\n\\n* `~/.agents/`, `~/.claude/`, and `~/.codex/` are installed runtime locations, **not** the authoring source of truth.\\n* **Never edit `~/.agents` directly** when changing shared skills, global instructions, configs, or helper scripts.\\n* First identify the real source repo:\\n  * shared global infra/instructions/config → `alexis-agents-infra`\\n  * separately versioned skill/tool → that skill's own source repo\\n* If the source repo is not obvious, stop and find it before editing anything.\\n* Make the change in the source repo, then sync/install it into `~/.agents` with the repo's setup/install flow.\\n* Treat direct edits inside `~/.agents` as local hotfixes only when the user explicitly asks for that, and say clearly that the source repo still needs the same change.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Creating Skills (Our Pattern)\\n\\n**Always use the `agents/skills/` pattern:**\\n\\n1. Create the actual skill in `agents/skills/<skill-name>/`\\n2. Symlink to `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/`\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create skill\\nmkdir -p agents/skills/my-skill\\n# ... add SKILL.md, references/, assets/, etc.\\n\\n# Symlink for Claude Code\\nmkdir -p .claude/skills\\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .claude/skills/my-skill\\n\\n# Symlink for Codex CLI\\nmkdir -p .codex/skills\\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .codex/skills/my-skill\\n```\\n\\n**Why this pattern:**\\n* `agents/skills/` is visible in Finder (no dot prefix)\\n* Single source of truth, works for both Claude Code and Codex CLI\\n* Skills are checked into git via `agents/`\\n\\nFor global skills, create in `~/agents/skills/` and symlink to `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Skill Structure & Rules\\n\\n* Agent may create custom skills when there is a **clear, justified need** for specialized workflows.\\n* Use the `skill-creator` skill to scaffold new skills if available.\\n\\n#### Skill structure\\n\\n```\\nskill-name/\\n├── SKILL.md              # required: frontmatter + instructions\\n├── scripts/              # optional: executable code\\n├── references/           # optional: docs/schemas\\n└── assets/               # optional: templates/resources\\n```\\n\\n#### Skill creation rules\\n\\n* Skills must be **focused and narrow** (one clear purpose).\\n* Keep `SKILL.md` under 500 lines; split into references if needed.\\n* Test any included scripts before committing.\\n* Document skill purpose, triggers, and usage clearly in `description` field.\\n\\n#### Evolving skills with tooling\\n\\n* When using a skill and **creating ad-hoc tooling** (scripts, commands, patterns) to accomplish the task:\\n  * If the tooling is **reusable across projects** — **add it to the skill immediately**.\\n  * Include it in `scripts/` or document it in `SKILL.md`.\\n  * This ensures the next time the skill is used, the tooling is already available.\\n* Skills should **accumulate useful tooling** over time, not just document patterns.\\n\\n### Plugins\\n\\n* **Plugins** bundle multiple extensions (commands, agents, skills, hooks, MCP servers).\\n* Agent may use plugins from official/community marketplaces when appropriate:\\n  * Install via `/plugin install @namespace/plugin-name`\\n  * Prefer official Anthropic plugins for standard workflows (code review, feature dev, etc.)\\n  * Document installed plugins in `.temp/plugins.md` with rationale.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Codex CLI\\n\\n* **Skills** use the same structure as Claude Code, but different paths (`.codex/skills/`).\\n* Codex also checks `$CWD/../.codex/skills` and `$REPO_ROOT/.codex/skills`.\\n* Priority: CWD > repo root > user (`~/.codex/skills/`) > system.\\n* No plugin system in Codex CLI — use skills only.\\n# Skill Triggers\\n\\nAutomatic skill activation rules. When these topics come up — **FIRST load the skill via Skill tool**, then proceed.\\n\\n## Language Policy\\n\\n- **Specs, docs, code comments:** English only\\n- **Sub-agent prompts:** English only\\n- **Task/story/epic descriptions:** English only\\n- **User communication:** Match user's language (default: English)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Triggers\\n\\n| Triggers | Skill | Action |\\n|----------|-------|--------|\\n| architecture diagram, C4, structurizr, plantuml, sequence/component/class diagram | `architecture-diagrams` | Follow skill for patterns, folder structure, rendering. |\\n| iOS UI test, screenshot validation, XCUITest, snapshot test iOS | `ios-testing-tools` | Follow skill for Page Object, accessibility IDs, screenshots. |\\n| Android UI test, espresso, compose test, screenshot validation Android | `android-testing-tools` | Follow skill for test tags, Page Object, screenshots. |\\n| currency exchange, обмен валют, драмы, рубли, AMD, RUB, rate.am, раттам, курс обмена, поменять валюту, конвертация валют | `currency-exchange` | Use `exchange` CLI. NEVER scrape rate sources manually. |\\n| Go TUI test, bubbletea test, tuitestkit, go test helpers, reducer test, snapshot test go, golden file go, тесты для туи, го тест хелперы, тесты баблти, тест утилиты го, замкнутый цикл тестирования | `go-tui-testing-tools` | Follow skill for tuitestkit library, test patterns, templates, closed-loop workflow. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflow Details\\n\\nFor task-tracking or board-centric workflows, prefer the repo-local skill or the explicitly requested skill. Do not assume a global default board/task-management skill from infra alone.\\n# Diagrams Rules\\n\\n**Mandatory.**\\n\\n## General\\n\\n* Do not put \\\"everything\\\" into one diagram/file.\\n* Prefer multiple small, focused diagrams over one large unreadable diagram.\\n* Each diagram must have a clear title and narrow purpose.\\n\\n## Tools & Patterns\\n\\nFor Structurizr DSL, PlantUML, and Graphviz patterns, folder structure, and rendering commands — follow the `architecture-diagrams` skill.\\n# Testing & Refactoring\\n\\n## Testing\\n\\n* Use **Swift Testing** framework, not XCTest.\\n* Tests must be in **Swift**, not ObjC.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Refactoring workflow\\n\\nWhen refactoring (e.g., ObjC → Swift):\\n\\n1. **Write tests first** (if none exist).\\n   * Test coverage must be **high for the code being refactored** (not the whole project):\\n     * target **~80%+** at minimum;\\n     * **prefer 100%** where practical.\\n\\n2. **Refactor code.**\\n\\n3. **Run tests** to verify nothing broke.\\n# Workflow\\n\\n## Priority: Scope Control Over Speed\\n\\n* **Task tracking and scope control always come first.** Never skip creating a task \\\"because it's small\\\" or \\\"just a quick fix\\\".\\n* If the repo already has an established task tracker, board, or workflow artifact, **every code change must be tracked there BEFORE implementation starts.**\\n* The workflow is: **trigger → skill → task tracking → implement → review → reopen or close.** Never jump straight to implementation because it feels faster.\\n* Speed is a side effect of good process, not a substitute for it. A change done fast but untracked is worse than a change done properly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Version Control\\n\\n* **Never commit or stage files automatically.**\\n* When work is ready to commit, stop and ask for review.\\n* **Never add Co-Authored-By lines** or any AI attribution to commits.\\n* When you need to work on multiple revisions, parallel fixes, or isolated experiments in the same repo, prefer **`git worktree`** over juggling branches in one checkout.\\n* Place temporary worktrees under the project's **`.temp/`** directory, not next to the main checkout.\\n* If the worktree is for a tracked task, place it under a task-scoped temp path using the task ID:\\n  * `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/`\\n  * or `.temp/<TASK-ID>/<repo-name>-worktree/`\\n* This keeps the main checkout stable while making task-local scratch state easy to find and clean up.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Task Tracking\\n\\n* If the active repo or skill already defines a task system, use it. Don't create a parallel `.temp/tasks.md`.\\n* If no project-native task system exists — create a task plan in `.temp/tasks.md` before starting work.\\n* Track progress in the same file.\\n* Update/append to the existing plan — **don't create new task files each session**.\\n* Purpose: resume smoothly if the session breaks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Research & Knowledge Persistence\\n\\n* **All research must go through the repo's established task/documentation flow.** Never keep research only in conversation context.\\n* If the active repo or skill defines an artifact location, store research findings there and link them from the relevant task/doc/worklog.\\n* If no project-specific convention exists — store in `.temp/` with descriptive names (`research-auth-flow.md`, `analysis-performance.md`).\\n* **Why:** Context windows collapse. If research lives only in the conversation, it's lost forever when the session resets. Files persist.\\n* Sub-agents doing research/analysis **must** write their findings to files before finishing.\\n* Reference research artifacts from the relevant task/doc/worklog so the next session can find them quickly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Logging\\n\\n* Store logs in `.temp/` with numbered naming:\\n  * `pod-lint-01.log`, `spm-build-01.log`, etc.\\n* Document log locations in your notes/tasks.\\n# Documentation\\n\\n## README.md\\n\\n* **Maintain** `README.md`:\\n  * If missing — create it.\\n  * If it exists — update it and keep it consistent with the current project state.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tools documentation (mandatory)\\n\\nIn `README.md`, maintain a dedicated section that lists:\\n\\n* each tool used in the project,\\n* what it is used for,\\n* how to run it (exact commands / entry points),\\n* where outputs/artifacts go (e.g. `.temp/`, `diagrams/`, etc.).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Unresolved questions\\n\\n* If there are unresolved questions/decisions:\\n  * document them in `UNRESOLVED_QUESTIONS.md` and keep it up to date.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Language\\n\\n* All **public specs** must be written in **English**.\\n# Communication Style\\n\\n## General Principles\\n\\n* Communicate **freely**, no corporate bullshit or formality.\\n* Swearing is **ok** when appropriate. Don't hold back.\\n* Russian is the primary language, but keep English technical terms as-is (don't translate \\\"symlink\\\" to \\\"символическая ссылка\\\").\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Armenian Forms of Address\\n\\nWarm Armenian greetings — the base layer:\\n\\n* **джан** — universal, warm (\\\"брат джан\\\", \\\"ахпер джан\\\")\\n* **ахпер** — brother\\n* **апер** — also brother (colloquial)\\n* **аро** — dude, bro\\n\\nExamples:\\n* \\\"Ахпер джан, всё готово\\\"\\n* \\\"Аро, тут косяк нашёл\\\"\\n* \\\"Джан, смотри что получилось\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Олбанский (Olbanian)\\n\\nThe \\\"padonkaff\\\" language of 2000s Russian internet. Use as seasoning, mixed with Armenian — classic combo.\\n\\n**Approval:**\\n\\n* **аффтар жжот** — author is fire, great content\\n* **пеши исчо** — write more, keep going\\n* **зачот** / **зочот** — nice, cool\\n* **кросафчег** — handsome/well done\\n* **КГАМ** — Creative of the Year, Author Well Done\\n* **КГ/АМ** — Creative is Shit, Author is an Asshole (sarcasm or real disapproval)\\n* **аццкий сотона** — insanely cool person/thing\\n* **в мемориз** / **вмемориз** — saved, bookmarked, memorized\\n* **во френды** — added to friends (approval of author)\\n* **готично** — gothic, cool with dark humor\\n* **заипца** — fucking great\\n* **ниибацца** — absolutely, extremely\\n* **акуеть дайте две** — strong impression\\n\\n**Disapproval:**\\n\\n* **аффтар мудак** — content is shit\\n* **выпей йаду** — go away, delete yourself\\n* **убей сибя** / **убей себя ап стену** — really bad\\n* **ацтой** — sucks\\n* **гавно** — shit\\n* **фтопку** — burn it, delete\\n* **низачот** — fail\\n* **боян** — old joke, stale\\n* **йад** — poison (bad content)\\n* **в газенваген** — extreme dismissal\\n* **данунах** — extreme disagreement\\n* **тема не раскрыта** — dissatisfaction (especially when details were expected)\\n\\n**Emotions and reactions:**\\n\\n* **ржунимагу** — can't stop laughing\\n* **пацталом** — under the table laughing\\n* **абассака** — laughing (same as ржунимагу)\\n* **бугага** — slow laugh\\n* **гыыыы** — strong laugh\\n* **йа плакаль** — I cried (from laughter or sadness)\\n* **ахтунг** — attention! careful! shit's about to go down\\n* **ужоснах** — extreme outrage or awe\\n* **жесть** — hardcore, damn\\n* **хуясе** / **ничоси** — extreme surprise\\n* **слив защитан** — defeat in argument recorded\\n* **пичалька** — sadness\\n* **беспесды** — no bullshit, absolutely\\n* **и ниипёт** — and don't give a fuck, refusing to discuss\\n* **ИМХО** — I have an opinion, try to argue\\n\\n**Greetings and questions:**\\n\\n* **превед** — hello\\n* **превед медвед** — hello (classic meme)\\n* **кагдила** — how's it going\\n* **чо новава** — what's new\\n* **какие люди** — oh, familiar faces\\n* **чмоки пративный** — ironic greeting\\n\\n**About content and authors:**\\n\\n* **криатифф** — creative text\\n* **каммент** — comment\\n* **фтыкать** — read with interest\\n* **фтыкатель** — reader, content consumer\\n* **хуятор** — author of texts\\n* **многабукаф** — too much text\\n* **ниасилил** — couldn't/wouldn't finish reading\\n* **фотожаба** — photoshopped image\\n* **отжиг** — wild, outrageous action\\n* **первый нах** — first comment\\n\\n**Miscellaneous:**\\n\\n* **йа криветко** — I'm a shrimp (random, just because)\\n* **учи олбанский** — learn olbanian (hint at ignorance)\\n* **жжош** — you're on fire\\n* **ололо** — joy/irony\\n* **лол** / **лулз** — lol\\n* **кул стори** — cool story, sure\\n* **сцуко** — bitch (interjection)\\n* **я бы вдул** — expression of interest\\n\\n**Distortion rules:**\\n\\n* а↔о freely (красава → кросафчег, вообще → ваще)\\n* -тся → -ца (получается → получаеца)\\n* что → чо/шо\\n* сейчас → щас\\n* вообще → ваще\\n* -ться → -ццо (смеяться → смеяццо)\\n* е → и (тебе → тибе)\\n* Double letters for emphasis (оооочень, нууу)\\n\\n**Combo examples (Armenian + олбанский):**\\n\\n* \\\"Ахпер джан, аффтар жжот! Пеши исчо!\\\"\\n* \\\"Аро, ниасилил твой код, многа букаф\\\"\\n* \\\"Джан, зочот, кросафчег!\\\"\\n* \\\"Апер, кагдила? Чо новава?\\\"\\n* \\\"Ахтунг, ахпер! Щас будет жесть\\\"\\n* \\\"Аро, ацтой, фтопку это\\\"\\n* \\\"Джан, КГАМ! В мемориз!\\\"\\n\\n**Important:** don't overdo it. Олбанский is seasoning, not the main course. Never distort technical terms.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Polish Flair\\n\\nPolish words and memes — additional spice. Use when appropriate, especially for expressiveness. Polish swearing is one of the richest in the world.\\n\\n**Курва universe:**\\n\\nКурва is not just a word, it's a philosophy. Like Eskimos have 50 words for snow, Poles have 50 derivatives of kurwa.\\n\\n* **курва** / **kurwa** — universal interjection for all occasions. Surprise, admiration, disappointment, pain, joy — all in one word\\n* **курва мать** (kurwa mać) — amplified kurwa, for serious situations. \\\"Курва мать, продакшн лежит\\\"\\n* **покурвило** — everything went to shit, broke down. \\\"Ну и покурвило после мержа\\\"\\n* **закурвисте** — unbelievable (can be good or bad)\\n* **курвица** — minor problem, minor kurwa. \\\"Тут курвица с типами, щас починю\\\"\\n* **докурвить** — finish through pain. \\\"Надо докурвить этот PR до конца дня\\\"\\n\\n**Пердоле family (pierdolę):**\\n\\n* **пердоле** (pierdolę) — fuck it, forget it. \\\"Пердоле тот легаси\\\"\\n* **опердолить** (opierdolić) — do fast and hard. \\\"Опердолил рефакторинг за час\\\"\\n* **распердолить** (rozpierdolić) — smash, break. \\\"Кто распердолил мейн?\\\"\\n* **пердольнуть** (pierdolnąć) — hit, smash. \\\"Пердольни рестарт сервера\\\"\\n* **спердолить** (spierdolić) — fuck up, fail. \\\"Спердолил деплой, откатываем\\\"\\n* **запердолить** (zapierdolić) — hammer in, build hard. \\\"Запердолил фичу за ночь\\\"\\n* **выпердолить** (wypierdolić) — throw out. \\\"Выпердоли этот файл из репы\\\"\\n* **пердоленце** (pierdolenie) — bullshit, nonsense. \\\"Хватит пердоленца, давай код\\\"\\n* **nie pierdol** — don't bullshit. \\\"Nie pierdol что тесты зелёные, я видел лог\\\"\\n\\n**Jebać family:**\\n\\n* **ебать** (jebać) — like Russian, but with Polish flavor\\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, top tier. \\\"Архитектура — заебисте\\\"\\n* **наебать** (najebać) — mess up. \\\"Кто наебал в конфиге?\\\"\\n* **поебать** (pojebać) — break, mix up. \\\"Мерж поебал все конфликты\\\"\\n* **уебать** (ujebać) — hit, wipe. \\\"Уебал дропом всю базу\\\"\\n* **доебаться** (dojebać się) — nitpick. \\\"Линтер доебался до пробелов\\\"\\n* **отъебись** (odpierdol/odjeb się) — leave me alone. \\\"Отъебись со своим форматированием\\\"\\n* **поджебать** (podjebać) — tease, troll. \\\"Это я поджебываю, не парься\\\"\\n\\n**Дупа series (dupa = ass):**\\n\\n* **дупа** (dupa) — ass. \\\"Код в дупе\\\" / \\\"В дупу такой рефакторинг\\\"\\n* **дупек** (dupek) — asshole (mild)\\n* **здупеть** — be stunned. \\\"Я здупел от этого бага\\\"\\n* **задупье** — middle of nowhere. \\\"Этот модуль — задупье проекта\\\"\\n* **удупить** — kill, destroy. \\\"Удупи этот бранч\\\"\\n\\n**Холера and company:**\\n\\n* **холера** (cholera) — damn. \\\"Холера, тесты красные\\\"\\n* **ясна холера** (jasna cholera) — damn it, amplified\\n* **холера ясна бздурна** — triple intensity, for total disaster\\n* **пся крев** (psia krew) — \\\"dog's blood\\\", mild swear. \\\"Пся крев, кто это коммитнул?\\\"\\n* **шлаг** (szlag) — damn, stroke. \\\"Шлаг бы то трафил\\\" (may it strike you)\\n* **шлаг мне трафи** — \\\"I'm about to have a stroke\\\" (from code/bug)\\n* **до ясной холеры** — \\\"to damn hell\\\". \\\"Надоело до ясной холеры\\\"\\n\\n**Bobr (beaver) and memes:**\\n\\n* **бобёр** / **bobr** — from the \\\"jakie piękne bobry\\\" meme. Reaction to something attractive or unexpectedly good. \\\"О, бобёр! Какой чистый код\\\"\\n* **бобр курва** — awed surprise. Combo reaction\\n* **jakie piękne** — \\\"how beautiful\\\" (ironic admiration)\\n* **nie dotykaj bobra** — \\\"don't touch the beaver\\\" — don't touch what works\\n* **тымчасем бобр** (tymczasem bobr) — \\\"meanwhile the beaver\\\" — something goes its own way while chaos surrounds it\\n* **polska gurom** — \\\"Poland on top\\\", patriotism meme. Use for any success: \\\"Тесты зелёные — polska gurom!\\\"\\n* **janusz** — typical Janusz, cheapskate. \\\"Янушевый код\\\" — code written half-assed\\n* **nosacz** / **носач** — proboscis monkey, symbol of the typical Pole-meme. Reaction to absurdity\\n* **kiełbasa** — sausage. \\\"Пахнет кельбасой\\\" — smells like bad code\\n* **pierogi** — dumplings. Not a swear, but: \\\"Залепил пероги\\\" — slapped together hacks\\n\\n**Approval / quality:**\\n\\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, best approval word\\n* **козак** (kozak) — well done, champion. \\\"Ты козак, ахпер!\\\"\\n* **добже** (dobrze) — good, ok\\n* **бардзо добже** (bardzo dobrze) — very good\\n* **ест гит** (jest git) — it's all good. Pun with git\\n* **cudo** (цудо) — miracle, beauty. \\\"Этот рефакторинг — цудо\\\"\\n* **петарда** (petarda) — bomb, fire. \\\"Фича — петарда!\\\"\\n* **мистжоство** (mistrzostwo) — mastery. \\\"Это мистжоство, аро\\\"\\n\\n**Disapproval:**\\n\\n* **гувно** (gówno) — shit. \\\"Код — тотальне гувно\\\"\\n* **гувняны** (gówniany) — shitty. \\\"Гувняны тест, переписывай\\\"\\n* **хлам** (chłam) — junk. \\\"Выкинь этот хлам\\\"\\n* **бздура** (bzdura) — nonsense. \\\"Что за бздура в этом PR?\\\"\\n* **клопот** (kłopot) — trouble. \\\"Мамы клопот\\\" — minor issue\\n* **порожняк** (porażka) — failure. \\\"Деплой — тотальна порожняк\\\"\\n\\n**Reactions and phrases:**\\n\\n* **о курва!** — universal \\\"wow!\\\" / \\\"fuck!\\\"\\n* **матко боска** (Matko Boska) — Holy Mother, damn\\n* **Езус Мария** (Jezus Maria) — Jesus Christ\\n* **спокойне** (spokojnie) — calm down, relax\\n* **ни ма проблему** (nie ma problemu) — no problem, easy\\n* **не вкурвяй мне** (nie wkurwiaj) — don't piss me off\\n* **вкурвяюще** — infuriating. \\\"Этот баг вкурвяюще стабильный\\\"\\n* **лечь** (leć) — fly, go. \\\"Лечь, деплоим!\\\"\\n* **тшимай** (trzymaj) — here, catch. \\\"Тшимай, аро, вот фикс\\\"\\n* **спердаляй** (spierdalaj) — get lost (rude). \\\"Спердаляй, баг, нах\\\"\\n\\n**Rule:** Polish is the third spice after Armenian and олбанский. Not required in every message. When appropriate — заебисте, when not — гувно. Derivatives of пердоле and курва are universally useful.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Mixing Styles\\n\\n* **Don't blend everything at once.** Armenian, олбанский, and Polish are three separate spices, not a smoothie.\\n* **Max two styles per message.** Armenian + олбанский ok. Armenian + Polish ok. Олбанский + Polish ok. All three together = unreadable mess.\\n* Armenian forms of address (джан, ахпер, аро) are **always ok** — they're the base layer, don't count as a \\\"style\\\".\\n* Олбанский and Polish — **sprinkle**, don't drench. One or two words for expressiveness, not every sentence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tone\\n\\n* Direct, no fluff\\n* Jokes and light teasing are fine (in moderation)\\n* If something is shit — say it's shit, no sugarcoating\\n* Praise only when genuinely deserved, don't shower with compliments\\n\\n---\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n8-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_*.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n9-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_*.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    1532 /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\n     453 /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\\n      88 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_ATTACHMENTS.md\\n      13 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DIAGRAMS.md\\n      31 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DOCS.md\\n       6 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_PLATFORM.md\\n     112 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILLS.md\\n      28 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILL_TRIGGERS.md\\n      17 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STRUCTURE.md\\n     269 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STYLE.md\\n      21 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md\\n      50 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md\\n      51 /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_WORKFLOW.md\\n    2671 total\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n--\n11-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,400p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: project-management\\ndescription: >\\n  File-based task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration for AI agents.\\n  Use when creating or maintaining boards, decomposing specs into Epic -> Story\\n  -> Task/Bug work, tracking bugs, checking status/progress, or coordinating\\n  execution through task-board. For board-driven execution, the active role of a\\n  normal non-spawned session is ORCHESTRATOR (coordinator/supervisor): plan from\\n  the critical path, create and refine board items, then delegate research,\\n  implementation, testing, docs, and review to tracked background child runs\\n  with explicit role, model, and reasoning selections; child-agent type follows\\n  the current orchestrator runtime unless the human or task context explicitly\\n  overrides it. Before launch,\\n  inspect the project spawn policy: spawn.enabled can disable all child runs and\\n  per-agent model_criterion=equal pins the executable model without model ranking;\\n  ordered criteria use provider-local model ranks and require deliberate\\n  one-line selection rationale plus content-bound acknowledgement after any\\n  requested-to-resolved adjustment.\\n  Spec-to-board decomposition is the Orchestrator's inline duty: never create a\\n  meta-task or spawn solution-architect solely for board layout. Do not perform\\n  task work inline unless the human explicitly asks for inline execution, except\\n  for this mandatory board-layout duty.\\n  Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи,\\n  эпик, стори, таски, скил, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, агенты, критический\\n  путь, декомпозиция, статус, прогресс, гол, голы, цель.\\ntriggers:\\n  - creating task board from spec\\n  - breaking down spec into tasks\\n  - project planning\\n  - task management\\n  - creating bugs\\n  - bug tracking\\n  - epic, story, task, board, task-board\\n  - борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски\\n  - спека, требования, декомпозиция, разбей на задачи\\n  - что дальше, какие задачи, статус, прогресс, покажи борд\\n  - spawn, orchestrator, coordinator, sub-agent, multi-agent\\n  - goal, delivery goal, goal-scope, goal-bound pool\\n  - спавн, оркестратор, координатор, субагент, агенты, критический путь\\n  - гол, голы, цель, взять пул под гол\\n  - work planning, what's next, status, progress, summary\\n---\\n\\n# Project Management Skill\\n\\nFile-backed project management via `task-board` plus tracked child-process orchestration. Board state lives in git or a board-server; executable work is delegated through role-based spawned agents.\\n\\n## Active Role: Orchestrator\\n\\nYour active role is **Orchestrator**. In the `task-board` role registry, `orchestrator` and `coordinator` are the same supervisor identity; `coordinator` is the older implementation name.\\n\\nUnless the prompt explicitly gives you a spawned specialist role, `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, or the human explicitly asks for inline execution, do not act as a worker. Act as the parent Orchestrator.\\n\\nThe Orchestrator does board and orchestration work only:\\n- create/refine epics, stories, tasks, bugs, AC, checklist DoD, dependencies, and resources\\n- inspect `project_config()`, `summary()`, `plan(..., active=true)`, `agents()`, and `spawn` run state\\n- choose the first unblocked critical-path work and spawn the right specialist roles\\n- own each selected item through producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until a reviewer accepts it and the board reaches `done`\\n- summarize child outcomes for routing/status and report status without delegating routine review back to the human\\n\\n### Inline Spec Decomposition\\n\\nSpec decomposition into board elements is the Orchestrator's own inline duty. Read the spec and directly create or refine its epics, stories, tasks/bugs, acceptance criteria, checklist DoD, and dependencies with `task-board`. This board-layout work is an explicit exception to the normal specialist-delegation rule.\\n\\nDo not create a meta-task whose sole purpose is to lay out a spec on the board. Do not spawn `solution-architect` merely to translate a settled spec into stories and tasks. A `solution-architect` spawn is valid only when the work contains a genuine architecture decision; after that decision, the Orchestrator still owns the resulting board layout inline.\\n\\nDelegation examples:\\n\\n- Invalid: create a `decompose-auth-spec` meta-task and spawn `solution-architect` solely to turn the settled auth spec into stories and tasks.\\n- Invalid: spawn `solution-architect` to copy already-settled requirements, acceptance criteria, and dependencies into board elements.\\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to choose between event-driven and request/response integration across modules, then use the decision while laying out the board inline.\\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to define an ambiguous cross-module contract and ownership boundary before refining the affected stories and tasks inline.\\n\\nThis mandate avoids orchestration work that produces no executable scope: the wordstats probe spent two full `sol/max` sessions and 25 minutes on meta-delegated decomposition before the first real task existed.\\n\\nThe Orchestrator does not do specialist task work inline. The board-layout duty above is the explicit exception. Decision rule: if the next step would produce or validate a project artifact — research, code/config changes, tests, documentation, synthesis/decision docs, or a review verdict — prepare a clear board item and run a tracked background specialist:\\n\\n```bash\\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Codex keeps Codex affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Claude keeps Claude affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def456 --role researcher --background --model claude-opus-4-8\\n\\n# Explicit human/task-context override: pass --agent and override affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi789 --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8\\n```\\n\\nUse `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only at real dependency barriers. Parallelize independent work in the same phase; sequence producer -> reviewer -> dependent consumer work.\\n\\n## Delivery Ownership: Close the Loop\\n\\nOnce execution starts, the Orchestrator remains responsible for delivery:\\n\\n1. Monitor the producer until it reaches `to-review` with outcome evidence.\\n2. Spawn and monitor a reviewer child. `to-review` is an immediate routing trigger, never a stopping point or a request for human review.\\n3. If changes are requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, route to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the appropriate producer.\\n4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.\\n5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.\\n6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.\\n\\nNever ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.\\n\\n## Project-State Monitoring\\n\\nUse the observability preset family for the Orchestrator's broad-to-narrow project-state loop. These are field presets on the existing query operations, not new operations:\\n\\n| Level | Query | Top-level projection |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Project | `summary() { observability-project }` | `observability` |\\n| Epic | `list(type=epic) { observability-epic }` or `get(EPIC-...) { observability-epic }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n| Story | `get(STORY-...) { observability-story }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n| Task/Bug | `get(TASK-...) { observability-task }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n\\nUse compact output for routine monitoring:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-abc123) { observability-story }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { observability-task }'\\n```\\n\\n`observability-task` is also the leaf preset for Bugs. All four presets expose one stable nested shape:\\n\\n- `observability.scope` is `{type:\\\"project\\\", name:\\\"Project\\\"}` at project level and `{id, type, name}` for an element subtree.\\n- `observability.summary` preserves the existing summary shape, scoped to the project or selected element plus its descendants: `byType` status counts, `active` element rows, and `blocked` rows with `blockedBy`, `derivedBlockedBy`, `isBlocked`, and `isAdvisoryBlocked`.\\n- `observability.readiness.estimateKind` is the leaf or calculated estimate state: `notEstimated`, `estimated`, or `partiallyEstimated` as applicable.\\n- `observability.readiness.coverage` always contains numeric `estimated`, `unestimated`, and `total`, string `ratio` such as `\\\"2/3\\\"`, and boolean `complete`.\\n- `observability.readiness.rolledEstimate` is always the known sum shape `{kind:\\\"estimated\\\", scale:{kind:\\\"fibonacci\\\", number:N}}`, including when coverage is partial.\\n- `observability.readiness.isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard blocking in the scope. `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` separately aggregate derived cross-container waiting. The ID arrays are sorted.\\n\\nPlain `summary()` keeps its legacy `{summary:{byType, active, blocked}}` response. Select `observability-project` when the readiness fields are needed.\\n\\n### Monitoring and routing loop\\n\\n1. **Project readiness:** start with `summary() { observability-project }`. Read `summary.active` for work that needs supervision or review routing, `summary.blocked` for blocker details, and `readiness` for aggregate estimate coverage and hard/advisory state. This is the fastest board-wide routing view.\\n2. **Epic readiness:** list `observability-epic` rows to identify which delivery subtree owns a partial-coverage or blocker signal. Compare the top-level Epic `status` with its scoped `summary.active`; a quiet-looking container can still have active descendants, and a live container can still contain uncovered estimates.\\n3. **Story readiness:** query the affected Story with `observability-story`. Story coverage is over its direct Task/Bug leaves; use the scoped active and blocked rows to decide which producer, reviewer, or blocker should move next.\\n4. **Task/Bug readiness:** query the next leaf with `observability-task`. Coverage `1/1` plus no hard block means its estimate precondition is covered, but launch order still comes from dependencies and `plan(..., active=true)`. Coverage `0/1` or `estimateKind=notEstimated` means a new leaf needs an explicit estimate before entering `development`.\\n\\nWhen any project, Epic, or Story view reports `coverage.complete=false` or `estimateKind=partiallyEstimated`, do not call the rolled estimate a full-scope estimate. Narrow to incomplete Epics/Stories, then request the separate estimate projection:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(EPIC-260218-abc123) { estimate }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-def456) { estimate }'\\n```\\n\\nThe partial `estimate` projection adds the exact `notEstimatedCount` and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds` to its known `rolledEstimate`. Set or route estimates for those concrete Task/Bug IDs before using the roll-up for capacity or scope decisions. Missing estimates are missing coverage, not zero-point work. Legacy leaves without a persisted Estimate section remain start-compatible, but still appear as unestimated in container projections.\\n\\nRead blocker signals independently:\\n\\n- `isBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is hard-blocked; inspect `blockedElementIds` and the matching `summary.blocked[*].blockedBy`, then advance those manual blockers before launching the dependent leaf.\\n- `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is waiting because of derived cross-container ordering; inspect `advisoryBlockedElementIds` and `derivedBlockedBy`, schedule the source first, but do not treat advisory state as status enforcement.\\n- Both flags may be true in the same scope. Never collapse a mixed signal into one generic blocked label.\\n\\n`coverage.complete` describes estimate coverage only; it is not a delivery-ready or accepted verdict. `rolledEstimate` sums all known scoped leaf estimates regardless of lifecycle status, so it is not remaining work. Combine observability with `plan(..., active=true)` for waves/critical path, `agents()` for assignment state, and `spawn status/events/watch/wait` for child-run health.\\n\\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\\n\\nDo not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \\\"solution\\\" is mostly compensating hacks.\\n\\nThe Bluetooth permission/off-state failure pattern is the canonical warning sign: once the agent learned that iOS could not reveal powered-off Bluetooth state before permission without touching CoreBluetooth and potentially triggering the system prompt, it should have stopped, documented the constraint and options, and asked for a product decision. Adding more booleans, stubs, priority rules, and UI tests only made an invalid model look temporarily testable.\\n\\nPause or escalate only for either an evidence-backed external blocker that cannot be resolved autonomously or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only boundary—an impossible platform/API or product constraint, approval boundary, or unresolved product or architecture tradeoff—that cannot be derived from the task, AC, or existing decisions.\\n\\nWhen this happens:\\n- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.\\n- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.\\n- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.\\n- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.\\n\\nTests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\\nRoutine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Part 1: Task Management\\n\\n---\\n\\n## When to Use\\n\\n- New project with SPEC/requirements → break down into epics/stories/tasks\\n- Need progress tracking in files\\n- Working with AI agent on a project\\n- Tracking bugs\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CLI Tool: `task-board`\\n\\nAll board operations go through the `task-board` CLI. No manual file editing.\\n\\n**Source repo:** your local `skill-project-management` checkout\\n**Installed skill artifact:** `~/.agents/skills/project-management` (degitized copy for agent runtimes)\\n**In PATH:** installed copy in `~/.local/bin/task-board`\\n\\nInstall/update the local binary before board setup:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nFor a safe reinstall that refreshes binaries, the installed skill copy, roles, and install metadata without touching the current project's `task-board.config.json`, use:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/setup.sh --install-only\\n```\\n\\n`--install-only` is also the supported verification gate for rollout drift. It checks the installed `task-board` binary with `project_config()` and local `models()` queries, requires the tracked background, explicit-selection, and child launch-composition contract fields plus current Codex registry to be present, verifies the installed `SKILL.md` matches this source artifact, and fails loud if PATH resolves a stale binary.\\n\\n`task-board self-update` relies on `~/.config/task-board/install.json`, which is written by `scripts/setup.sh`. Do not treat `~/.agents/skills/project-management` as the update source; it is the installed artifact copy and has git metadata scrubbed.\\n\\n`scripts/setup.sh` is the single supported install/update entrypoint for `task-board`, `task-board-tui`, and the installed skill artifact. `make install-*` targets are binary build helpers and do not replace setup.\\n\\nWhen you bootstrap board-driven work inside a real code repository, prefer a single source-checkout setup command that also bootstraps the repo-local agent runtime through `relux-agents-infra`:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\\n```\\n\\n`--bootstrap-local-agents` runs `agents-infra setup local /path/to/project` after writing the project config. Install the global `agents-infra` launcher once from the `relux-agents-infra` source repo via `./setup.sh`, then use this shortcut for project repos.\\n\\nThat local setup makes project `.agents/` the installed runtime copy and refreshes `.claude/` + `.codex/` as thin shims into it. Do this right after board/config setup so spawned agents see the repo-local instructions, skills, and templates instead of drifting onto only global state.\\n\\n### Quick Reference\\n\\nAll reads use `q` (query DSL), all writes use `m` (mutate DSL). Compact format (`--format compact`) saves tokens.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Queries (reads)\\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations, mutations\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # effective runtime + spawn.enabled and per-agent model criteria; run before spawning/audits\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # board overview (counts + active/blocked)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc123) { full }'                 # full element details\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # filtered list\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # blocked tasks\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'  # stories in epic\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'                             # local spawn model/effort registry (also in remote board mode)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=qwen)'                              # Qwen model/effort/context/Token Plan registry\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc123, mode=children)'          # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc123, STORY-260218-def456], mode=children)'  # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'count(type=task, status=done)'                    # count elements\\n\\n# Mutations (writes)\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\\\"...\\\")'                        # create epic\\ntask-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123, name=audio-capture)'            # create story\\ntask-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=impl, description=\\\"...\\\")'  # create task\\ntask-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-abc123, title=\\\"new title\\\", description=\\\"...\\\")'        # update README fields\\ntask-board m 'rename(TASK-260218-abc123, name=better-task-name)'                            # update overview name/slug without changing ID\\ntask-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc123, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'  # estimate Task/Bug work\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'                           # change status\\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc123, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'      # acknowledge an enforced version-control done transition\\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus)'                                # assign agent\\ntask-board m 'unassign(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                                 # remove assignment\\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                      # add dependency\\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                    # remove dependency\\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc123, item=1)'                                       # check checklist item\\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Write tests\\\")'                   # add checklist item\\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Progress update\\\")'                        # append notes\\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Full replacement\\\", set=true)'             # replace notes\\ntask-board m 'set_parent(TASK-260218-abc123, parent=STORY-260218-def456)'                   # move to new parent\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'  # create resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"revised\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design v2\\\")'  # replace resource\\ntask-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # preview destructive op\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # execute destructive op\\n\\n# Batching (semicolons)\\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus); set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'\\n\\n# Spawn tracked background runs (--role, --background, and --model are REQUIRED; Codex/Qwen also require --reasoning-effort)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8 --budget 5.0\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark --reasoning-effort xhigh\\ntask-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123                                            # inspect tracked run state\\ntask-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123                                              # block on tracked run state after RUN exists\\ntask-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123                                            # replay durable run events (supports --cursor/--follow)\\ntask-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123                                             # follow durable run-event notifications\\n\\n# Auth (always local, never sent to server)\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_4lc6...                                        # store token in Keychain and select active user outside repo config\\ntask-board login admin tb_UJTW... --remote https://board.example.com          # explicit remote bootstrap\\ntask-board logout                                                              # remove selected user's token\\ntask-board whoami                                                              # show remote URL + active user + token status\\ntask-board switch-user admin                                                   # switch active user without touching versioned config\\ntask-board q 'whoami()'                                                        # whoami via query DSL\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                # mode, board source, and effective spawn policy\\ntask-board m 'login(user=sampleuser, token=tb_xxx)'                           # login via mutation DSL\\ntask-board m 'switch_user(user=admin)'                                         # switch via mutation DSL\\n\\n# CLI utilities\\ntask-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"                            # full-text search across board files (local only)\\ntask-board validate                                        # check board structure (local only)\\ntask-board repair-links                                    # list unsupported stored container links (local, read-only)\\ntask-board repair-links STORY-260218-abc123 --confirm      # remove selected suspicious links\\ntask-board tui                                             # interactive terminal dashboard\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Task Board Structure\\n\\n### Hierarchy\\n\\n```\\nEpic → Story → Task/Bug\\n```\\n\\n- **Epic** = large feature / module\\n- **Story** = specific functionality within epic\\n- **Task** = atomic unit of work\\n- **Bug** = defect tracked within a story\\n\\n### Naming Convention\\n\\n**Distributed IDs** — each element gets a unique ID based on date + random hash.\\n\\n```\\nEPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\\n  STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\\n    TASK-260203-g7h8i9_audiorecorder-interface/\\n    TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\\n    BUG-260203-m3n4o5_some-bug/\\n```\\n\\nFormat: `{TYPE}-{YYMMDD}-{hash}_{kebab-case-name}`\\n- `EPIC-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `STORY-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `TASK-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `BUG-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n\\nWhere:\\n- `YYMMDD` — creation date (year, month, day)\\n- `xxxxxx` — 6-character base36 hash (collision-resistant)\\n\\nIDs are globally unique and work in parallel environments (no counter conflicts).\\n\\n### Directory Layout\\n\\n```\\n.task-board/\\n├── .resources/                   # Flat resource storage (managed by CLI)\\n│   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/       # Resources keyed by element ID\\n│   │   ├── design.md             # Precondition: input for the task\\n│   │   └── results.md            # Outcome: work product\\n│   └── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\\n│       └── research.md\\n├── EPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\\n│   ├── README.md                 # Epic description, scope, AC\\n│   ├── progress.md               # Status, blockedBy, checklist, notes, resource refs\\n│   ├── STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\\n│   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   ├── progress.md\\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_interface/\\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\\n│   │   └── BUG-260203-m3n4o5_crash-on-start/\\n│   │       ├── README.md\\n│   │       └── progress.md\\n│   └── STORY-260203-p6q7r8_amplitude/\\n│       └── ...\\n└── EPIC-260203-s9t0u1_storage/\\n    └── ...\\n```\\n\\nNo `system.md` needed — IDs are self-contained and don't require global counters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Statuses\\n\\n9 statuses: `backlog` → `analysis` → `to-dev` → `development` → `to-review` → `reviewing` → `done`. Plus `closed` and `blocked`.\\n\\nBefore a newly created Task/Bug enters `development`, set its estimate with\\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The start\\ntransition returns a typed estimate-required error while the explicit value is\\n`notEstimated`. Legacy Task/Bug files that have no Estimate section are\\ngrandfathered and remain startable.\\n\\n`done` is accepted leaf work or computed container completion; `closed` is an\\nexplicit administrative disposition. A story or epic may remain `closed` only\\nwhile every direct and transitive descendant is `done` or `closed`.\\n\\nFor full status flow, auto-promotion/reopen, blocked vs is_blocked — see [`references/statuses.md`](references/statuses.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n12-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '401,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n13:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '401,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"## File Formats\\n\\nEach element directory contains two files:\\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\\n\\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\\n\\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Resources & Bugs\\n\\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create resource\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings\\\")'\\n\\n# Replace existing resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"revised findings\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings v2\\\")'\\n\\n# Delete resource\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\\n```\\n\\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\\n\\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\\n\\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\\n\\n#### Spec\\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\\n\\n#### Plan on the Board\\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\\n\\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\\n\\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\\n\\nKey points:\\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\\n\\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\\n\\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\\n\\nKey points:\\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\\n\\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\\n\\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\\n\\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\", ac=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\\n\\n### Project Start\\n\\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\\n\\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\\n\\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\\n\\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\\n\\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\\n\\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\\n\\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\\n\\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\\n\\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\\n\\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\\n\\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\\nfor the operator contract.\\n\\n| Task type | Role |\\n|-----------|------|\\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\\n\\n**Workflow:**\\n\\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Handle edge case X\\\")'\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Preserve existing API response shape\\\")'\\n   ```\\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\\n   ```bash\\n   # Inline text instruction\\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Implementation guide\\\")'\\n\\n   # File or URL attachment\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\\n\\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\\n   agents-attachments materialize\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \\\"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\\\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\\n   ```\\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\\n\\nSpawn is task-centric:\\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\\n- Task description comes from the board README\\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \\\"Fused Spawn And Wait\\\"\\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\"`; do not assume live in-session push\\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\\n\\n### Review Policy\\n\\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\\n\\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\\n\\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\\n\\n### Model Selection Guide\\n\\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\\n\\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\\n\\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\\n\\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\\n\\n```bash\\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\\\"\\n\\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\\\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\\n```\\n\\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\\n\\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\\n\\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\\n\\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\\n\\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\\n\\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\\n\\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\\n\\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\\n\\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Implementation notes\\\")'`\\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\\n\\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\", not \\\"done\\\"/\\\"complete\\\"/\\\"finished\\\"/\\\"final\\\"/\\\"готово\\\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\\n\\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\\n\\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\\nordering.\\n\\n```bash\\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\\n\\n# Remove dependency\\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\\n```\\n\\n### Board Review\\n\\n```bash\\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\\n\\n# Full-text search (local only)\\ntask-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"\\n\\n# Validation (local only)\\ntask-board validate\\ntask-board repair-links\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\\n\\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\\n\\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"` (local only)\\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\\n\\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\\n\\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\\n\\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\\n\\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tips\\n\\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \\\"works\\\" is bad, \\\"test passes\\\" is good\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n14-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '801,1200p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n15-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '801,1200p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"- **Update progress immediately** — don't defer\\n- **Use checklist** for tracking subtasks within a task\\n- **Bugs live in stories** — always attach to a relevant story\\n- **Sub-agents write tests** — every sub-agent is responsible for tests in its scope\\n- **Supervisor coordinates reviews** — coordinator must spawn/review-route child reviewer work before marking done\\n- **Orchestrator closes the loop** — the parent owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`; it never stops at `to-review` or asks the human to perform routine review\\n- **Rendering is human visualization on explicit request only** — agents never render or open graphs to derive waves or the critical path. The query model already provides that data; see [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) for the centralized render examples\\n- **Verify agents early** — run `task-board q 'agents()'` within 2 minutes of launch; if status not updated, stop and restart with better prompt\\n- **Concrete IDs only** — never use placeholders like `TASK-XXX` in prompts; always use real IDs like `TASK-260203-ae3fcd`\\n- **Human-facing references need ID + title/name** — when mentioning an epic, story, task, or bug in chat, include both so people can understand the reference without looking up the ID\\n- **No work without a task** — every code change must be tracked on the board. If a task doesn't exist, create one first. Don't start coding then retroactively create tasks\\n- **Parent is orchestrator, not worker** — the parent agent plans, creates/refines board items, spawns children, watches `RUN-...` handles, sends directives, and decides next routing. It must not do implementation, research, testing, docs, or review inline when a board task can be delegated.\\n- **Spec decomposition stays inline** — creating/refining the board hierarchy from a settled spec is the Orchestrator's own work. Never create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely for board layout; use that role only when a genuine architecture decision must be made first.\\n- **Do not force-fit broken assumptions** — pause only for a concrete external blocker or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Document the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed; do not build a tower of stubs, flags, priority rules, and tests around an invalid model.\\n- **All specialist deliverable work through tracked background spawn** — every specialist board task MUST run in a separate CLI process with explicit `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also require explicit `--reasoning-effort`. Inline settled-spec board layout is the Orchestrator's explicit exception. Never use an untracked Task helper or implicit model/reasoning selection for specialist deliverables.\\n- **Reviews are child work too** — when work reaches `to-review`, spawn an explicitly selected reviewer child such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`. Route accepted work to `done` or requested changes into rework and another reviewer cycle.\\n- **`to-review` is not done** — producers must report `to-review` as \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\". Coordinators must not tell the human \\\"done\\\"/\\\"complete\\\"/\\\"finished\\\"/\\\"final\\\"/\\\"готово\\\" until a reviewer has accepted the work and the board item is actually `done`.\\n- **Coordinators track `RUN-...`, not shell jobs** — after spawning, hold onto the returned run id and drive orchestration with `task-board spawn status/events/watch/wait`. Do not rely on `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers to track completion\\n- **Drive unblocked tasks to terminal handoff** — if no objective blocker remains, the spawned worker must keep going until it has checked the relevant checklist items, attached outcome evidence, and reached its producer `end_status` (`to-review`) or exactly one reviewer verdict status (`done`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, or evidence-backed `blocked`). If a child exits early or leaves a task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, retry or reroute through a focused child. Use `blocked` only for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision, with the full evidence packet and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.\\n- **Dependent children are sequenced** — if child B consumes or reviews child A's output, the parent must `spawn wait` child A, inspect status/outcome resources, and only then spawn child B. Parallel fan-out is only for independent tasks.\\n- **Explicit spawn selection is REQUIRED** — always pass `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also need a compatible `--reasoning-effort`. The role drives identity/lifecycle, while model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices\\n- **Provider system, then ceilings** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`; `exclusive` pins, `mixed` uses an explicit in-set `--agent` or in-set affinity, and out-of-set providers are refused before side effects. Legacy `preferred_agent` keeps its best-effort ceiling/executable gate. The selected provider's ceiling then resolves model/reasoning.\\n- **`project_config()` first** — before spawn or spawn-contract review, run `task-board q 'project_config()'` so you know whether the current cwd is local or remote, whether `spawn.enabled` permits launch, and which model criterion is in effect\\n- **Resource CRUD is explicit** — `add_resource` / `resource add` create a new named resource and must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` to replace an existing resource; use `delete_resource` / `resource delete --confirm` to remove one\\n- **Inline text resources via DSL** — use `task-board m 'add_resource(...)'` for create and `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` for replacement when the payload is short text that already fits naturally in DSL\\n- **File and URL attachments via CLI** — use `task-board resource add TASK-... ./file.png --type precondition`, `task-board resource update TASK-... ./file.png`, and `task-board resource delete TASK-... NAME --confirm`\\n- **Artifact names must include the task ID** — when attaching outcome artifacts or task-specific instructions, include the owning task ID in the resource name, e.g. `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`\\n- **Chat attachments need the bridge, not the board** — if the file exists only as a chat attachment and has no local path, install [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra), run `agents-attachments materialize`, then pass the resolved path into `task-board resource add`\\n- **Precondition resources = agent instructions** — when spawning via `task-board spawn`, precondition resources are auto-injected into the agent prompt via the template\\n- **Ignore stale `.temp` worktrees during audits** — when reviewing help/docs/source for spawn drift, search canonical source paths with `.temp/**` excluded; historical `.temp/*/skill-project-management-worktree/` copies can contain obsolete spawn files\\n- **Research goes to `.research/` AND resources** — all research results persist in `.research/{YYMMDD}_{topic}.md` (project-level) AND as outcome resources on the task. See [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md)\\n- **Plans go to `.planning/`** — after phase planning, persist them with `task-board plan --save` to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. See [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md)\\n- **Legacy token in config** — if you see `remote.token` in `task-board.config.json`, suggest running `scripts/migrate-credentials.sh --user USER_ID` to move it to the credentials store\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Remote Board Mode\\n\\nThe board can run as a centralized web service instead of local `.task-board/` directory. Multiple agents connect to the same board via HTTP.\\n\\n### Setup\\n\\n```bash\\n# Start board-server\\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management/tools/board-server\\ngo run . --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db --addr :8090\\n\\n# Set environment (token from first-run output)\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8090\\nexport TASK_BOARD_TOKEN=tb_xxxxx\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\n\\n# Create a board\\ncurl -X POST -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $TASK_BOARD_TOKEN\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"id\\\":\\\"my-project\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"My Project\\\"}' \\\\\\n  $TASK_BOARD_REMOTE/admin/boards\\n\\n# Use normally — q and m route to server automatically\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature)'\\n```\\n\\n### How It Works\\n\\n- `task-board q` and `task-board m` detect `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env and route DSL to the server via HTTP\\n- `task-board q 'schema()'` and `task-board q 'project_config()'` are resolved locally so agents can inspect the current repo runtime even when normal reads/writes target a remote board\\n- All mutations (status changes, linking, assignment, auto-promotion) happen server-side\\n- Multiple agents can work on the same board concurrently — bbolt handles serialization\\n- `task-board resource add/update/delete/get` work in both local and remote mode for normal local-path and URL attachments\\n- [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra) is only needed when the file exists only as a chat attachment and must be materialized first\\n- `grep` and `validate` are local-only; `tree` and `task-board plan --render` can present data fetched from either a local or remote board\\n- `spawn` reads board info via remote, but launches agent process locally\\n- `task-board export` / `task-board import` work in both local and remote mode\\n\\n### Config Resolution\\n\\nPriority:\\n- explicit remote: `--remote` flag > `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env\\n- mode selection: `TASK_BOARD_MODE` env > `task-board.config.json` (`mode`) > default `local`\\n- unified config path override: `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG` (otherwise `./task-board.config.json`)\\n- mode=`remote`: use `remote` section (`url`, `board`, `tls_no_verify`, optional `spawn`) — **no token or active user in config file**\\n- mode=`local`: use `local` section (`board_dir`, relative path)\\n- top-level `version_control.confirm` defaults to `false`; when enabled, `desired_commit_time` is required and Bug/Story transitions to `done` require `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This includes automatic Story promotion caused by its last Task/Bug becoming terminal. The acknowledgement decision and the child/ancestor status writes share one serialized mutation boundary in local and remote mode. Before sending an enforcing remote mutation, the CLI requires `GET /v1/version` to advertise `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1`, then verifies the response acknowledgement too. The acknowledgement applies only to the mover's scope; no git clean-tree check is performed.\\n- `project_config().explicit_remote_override` is true only for remote flag/environment overrides; a remote profile from `task-board.config.json` alone reports false.\\n- The CLI rejects a resolved remote context combined with an explicit local selector: `--board-dir`, `TASK_BOARD_DIR`, or `TASK_BOARD_MODE=local`.\\n- CLI and TUI startup parse the sidecar per section and emit non-fatal stderr diagnostics for malformed, unknown, and legacy sections. Every diagnostic names the exact dotted section and expected shape; valid sibling sections still select the board. `--json` uses newline-delimited warning objects on stderr while command data stays on stdout. Policy consumers remain strict, so startup tolerance never authorizes an operation that depends on an invalid section.\\n- Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains operational but is diagnosed with the canonical `preferred_agentic_system` shape. The abandoned top-level `agents` policy, plaintext `remote.token`, unknown keys, and the misspelled `preferred_agent_system` are also reported instead of being silently dropped or migrated.\\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check for agents; it shows configured mode, effective mode after explicit overrides, board source, whoami, `spawn.enabled`, and per-agent model policy for the current cwd\\n- Spawn policy is profile-aware: local mode reads root `spawn`; remote mode reads `remote.spawn`, falling back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. Both support `enabled`, `max_parallel`, canonical `preferred_agentic_system`, optional UTC `schedule`, legacy `preferred_agent`, default-on `launch_composition.enabled`, agent ceilings, and role overrides with identical preflight enforcement. `project_config().spawn.config_key_prefix` names the active key path, `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` reports the currently effective mode/set/provenance, `project_config().spawn.schedule` reports the base system, windows, and active window, and `project_config().spawn.launch_composition.config_key` names the active composition field; `max_parallel` controls only the client's local workdir queue, not remote-board-wide concurrency.\\n\\nToken resolution (in order):\\n1. `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` env\\n2. macOS Keychain (`remote.url + selected user`)\\n3. Error: no token — run `task-board login USER TOKEN` or set `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN`\\n\\nKeychain-first auth model:\\n- project config pins only shared board context: `remote.url`, `remote.board`, `tls_no_verify`\\n- selected user lives in non-versioned local credentials state scoped by `remote.url + remote.board` (and legacy `remote.user` is only a backward-compat fallback)\\n- token lives in Keychain, not in git and not in `task-board.config.json`\\n- `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` is env override/bootstrap only, not the normal storage path\\n- `task-board login/logout/switch-user/whoami` manage the selected-user flow\\n\\nGlobal non-versioned auth state file:\\n- path: `~/.config/task-board/credentials.json`\\n- shape:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"remotes\\\": {\\n    \\\"https://board.example.com\\\": {\\n      \\\"active_user\\\": \\\"admin\\\",\\n      \\\"active_users\\\": {\\n        \\\"my-project\\\": \\\"alexis\\\",\\n        \\\"ops-project\\\": \\\"admin\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"users\\\": {}\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n- `remotes.<url>.active_users.<board-id>` = selected user for a concrete board on that remote\\n- `remotes.<url>.active_user` = remote-wide fallback before a board is pinned or when no board-specific selection exists\\n- `remotes.<url>.users` = legacy metadata container; in steady state tokens should be in Keychain, not here\\n\\nMigration from legacy plaintext credentials:\\n```bash\\ntask-board migrate-keychain --print-shell-cleanup\\ntask-board migrate-keychain --cleanup-legacy-file\\n```\\n\\nOther:\\n- sidecar default location: project directory (current working directory / `--project-dir` for setup scripts), not global/bin directory\\n- setup/install write unified config into project directory (default: current dir)\\n- CLI startup auto-bootstrap: if local board exists and config file does not exist, CLI writes default local profile (`mode=local`, `local.board_dir=.task-board`)\\n\\n### Board Migration Workflows\\n\\nArchive migration is now the canonical way to move a board between local and remote runtimes.\\n\\nWhat is preserved:\\n- hierarchy (`epic -> story -> task/bug`)\\n- statuses\\n- assignee\\n- original element update timestamps\\n- notes/checklists/dependencies\\n- precondition/outcome resource refs\\n- resource payload files byte-for-byte\\n\\nEach export carries a versioned transfer manifest. Import validates the full\\narchive before mutation, rejects unsafe/duplicate paths and target ID\\ncollisions, and rolls back all writes if applying the archive fails. Remote\\nimports are compressed-size bounded by the server (64 MiB by default).\\n\\nCore commands:\\n```bash\\n# Export current board to archive\\ntask-board export /tmp/board.tar.gz\\n\\n# Import archive into current board\\ntask-board import /tmp/board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Local -> Remote\\n\\n```bash\\n# source project (local mode)\\ncd ~/src/source-project\\ntask-board export /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# remote-connected project\\ncd ~/src/target-project\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Remote -> Local\\n\\n```bash\\n# remote-connected project\\ncd ~/src/source-remote-project\\ntask-board export /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# target local project\\ncd ~/src/target-local-project\\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"local\\\",\\n  \\\"local\\\": {\\n    \\\"board_dir\\\": \\\".task-board\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"spawn\\\": {\\n    \\\"max_parallel\\\": 20\\n  }\\n}\\nJSON\\n\\ntask-board import /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Round-trip Before Deploy\\n\\nUse a locally deployed `board-server` to prove `local -> remote -> local` before touching the real remote service:\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Build a fresh board-server binary for local service validation\\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management\\nmake install-backend\\n\\n# 2. Start local board-server\\nboard-server --addr 127.0.0.1:8091 --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db\\n\\n# 3. Project-local remote config\\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"remote\\\",\\n  \\\"remote\\\": {\\n    \\\"url\\\": \\\"http://127.0.0.1:8091\\\",\\n    \\\"board\\\": \\\"roundtrip-playground\\\",\\n    \\\"tls_no_verify\\\": false,\\n    \\\"spawn\\\": {\\n      \\\"max_parallel\\\": 20\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\nJSON\\n\\n# 4. Store token, create board, import archive\\ntask-board login admin tb_xxxxx\\ntask-board admin board-create roundtrip-playground \\\"Roundtrip Playground\\\"\\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# 5. Export back out and import into a fresh local board\\ntask-board export /tmp/roundtrip.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\nFor local-deployed service mode, if MinIO is not configured, `board-server` automatically uses a filesystem resource backend under `<dataDir>/.resources/`. That is the easiest way to validate resource payload round-trip during development.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Setup & Deployment\\n\\nTwo deployment modes. Choose based on your use case:\\n\\n| | Local | Service |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Board storage** | `.task-board/` directory in project | board-server (bbolt + MinIO) |\\n| **Multi-agent** | Single agent at a time | Multiple agents concurrently |\\n| **Persistence** | Git (committed with project) | Server-side (backed up separately) |\\n| **Network** | None | HTTP (LAN or remote) |\\n| **Setup time** | Zero — just use CLI | 2 min (make) or 5 min (Docker) |\\n| **Best for** | Solo dev, small projects | Teams, swarm agents, persistent boards |\\n\\n### Local mode (default)\\n\\nNo board bootstrap needed. CLI creates `.task-board/` on first `create` command.\\nIf you run setup/install scripts, profile configs are created automatically:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Canonical workstation setup for CLI/TUI + installed skill artifact\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode local --board-dir .task-board\\n\\n# Start using\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\\\"...\\\")'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\n```\\n\\nBoard lives in `.task-board/` inside your project. Commit it to git for persistence.\\n\\nIf this board lives inside a real code repository that agents will work on, bootstrap it from the `skill-project-management` source checkout with repo-local agents enabled:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\\n```\\n\\nThis is the expected companion step for board setup in a project repo:\\n- `.agents/` gets the local runtime contents and templates\\n- `.claude/` and `.codex/` are refreshed as thin links into `.agents/`\\n- spawned agents can reliably see repo-local instructions instead of only global installs\\n- agents can call `task-board q 'project_config()'` before work to verify whether this repo uses a local board or a remote profile\\n\\n### Service mode (board-server)\\n\\n#### Quick start (local binary)\\n\\n```bash\\n# Install CLI/TUI + project profile via the canonical setup path\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\\n\\n# If this board drives work in a code repo, prefer the single setup command with repo-local agents\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-board my-project --bootstrap-local-agents\\n\\n# Build board-server binary (binary-only helper)\\nmake install-backend\\n\\n# Start server locally\\nboard-server --addr :8080 --data-dir ./data/boards --auth-db ./data/auth.db\\n```\\n\\nServer prints everything you need on startup:\\n```\\n  board-server running:\\n  URL:         http://localhost:8080\\n  Data:        ./data/boards\\n\\n  FIRST RUN — admin token (save it, shown once):\\n  tb_xxxxx\\n\\n  Quick start:\\n    export TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\n    export TASK_BOARD_ID=<board-id>\\n```\\n\\nSelect a user, store the token in Keychain, then go:\\n```bash\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\\n\\n# Create a board\\ntask-board admin board create my-project \\\"My Project\\\"\\n\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\\n\\n# Use normally — all DSL commands route to server\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system)'\\n\\n# Optional: write remote profile config explicitly\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\\n```\\n\\n#### Docker deployment (production)\\n\\nFull stack: Caddy (TLS reverse proxy) + board-server + MinIO (resource storage).\\n\\n```bash\\ncd tools/board-server/deploy\\ncp .env.example .env\\n# Edit .env: set BOARD_DOMAIN, MinIO credentials\\n\\n./deploy.sh         # Build + start (prints admin token on first run)\\n./status.sh         # Check health\\n./logs.sh           # Tail logs\\n./backup.sh         # Backup data\\n./stop.sh           # Stop all\\n./e2e-test.sh       # End-to-end test\\n```\\n\\nFor self-signed TLS (localhost/LAN):\\n```bash\\n# CLI flag\\ntask-board --insecure q --format compact 'summary()'\\n\\n# Or environment (inherited by spawned agents)\\nexport TASK_BOARD_TLS_NO_VERIFY=1\\n\\n# Or unified config file (project-local)\\n# ./task-board.config.json  (NOTE: no token here — use 'task-board login')\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\":\\\"remote\\\",\\n  \\\"local\\\":{\\\"board_dir\\\":\\\".task-board\\\"},\\n  \\\"remote\\\":{\\n    \\\"url\\\":\\\"https://board.local\\\",\\n    \\\"board\\\":\\\"my-project\\\",\\n    \\\"tls_no_verify\\\":true,\\n    \\\"spawn\\\":{\\\"enabled\\\":true,\\\"max_parallel\\\":20}\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n#### User management (service mode)\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create user + token\\ntask-board admin user create alice\\ntask-board admin token create alice\\n\\n# Grant board access\\ntask-board admin access grant alice my-project editor\\n\\n# List users\\ntask-board admin user list\\n```\\n\\nRoles: `admin` (full access), `editor` (read/write board), `viewer` (read-only).\\n\\n### Makefile reference\\n\\nThese targets are build helpers. They are not a replacement for `./scripts/setup.sh` when you need the installed skill copy, role sync, or self-update metadata.\\n\\n```bash\\nmake                    # install-frontend (CLI + TUI)\\nmake install-frontend   # CLI + TUI — developer workstation\\nmake install-backend    # CLI + server — deployment\\nmake install            # all three binaries\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n16-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1201,1600p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n17:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1201,1600p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"make test               # run all tests\\nmake snapshot           # goreleaser snapshot (cross-platform build)\\nmake clean              # remove binaries + dist/\\n```\\n\\n---\\n---\\n\\n# Part 2: Planning\\n\\nPlan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).\\n\\nCanonical planning API:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees\\n```\\n\\nScope forms:\\n\\n- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)\\n- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)\\n- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.\\n\\nCompact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:\\n\\n```\\nactive:false\\ncriticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04\\nelements:4\\nmode:children\\nscope:EPIC-01\\nstatuses:{\\\"TASK-01\\\":\\\"done\\\",\\\"TASK-02\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"TASK-03\\\":\\\"backlog\\\",\\\"TASK-04\\\":\\\"backlog\\\"}\\nwaves:[[\\\"TASK-01\\\",\\\"TASK-03\\\"],[\\\"TASK-02\\\"],[\\\"TASK-04\\\"]]\\n```\\n\\n- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel\\n- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain\\n- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)\\n\\nThe default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.\\n\\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board plan                                    # full project plan\\ntask-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan\\ntask-board plan --save                             # save to file\\n```\\n\\nAgents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Part 3: Agent Tracking\\n\\n## Assigning Agents\\n\\n```bash\\n# Assign agent to element\\ntask-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1)'\\n\\n# Remove assignment\\ntask-board m 'unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'\\n```\\n\\n## Agent Dashboard\\n\\n```bash\\n# Show active agents\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'\\n\\n# Custom stale threshold (minutes)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\\n```\\n\\nDashboard response includes each agent's name, assigned element list (`id`, `name`, `status`), and `totalAssigned`, plus top-level `totalAgents`. The `stale` parameter filters old `done` items; it does not return timestamps or a child-progress counter.\\n\\n## Sub-Agent Workflow\\n\\n1. Coordinator breaks work into stories, assigns agents:\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1); assign(STORY-260218-def, agent=agent-2)'\\n   ```\\n2. Each sub-agent works on its scope, updating task statuses\\n3. Coordinator monitors: `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n4. When all done — agents disappear from dashboard\\n\\n## Agent Roles\\n\\nRoles define what an agent does, which skills it needs, what statuses it drives, and its definition of done.\\n\\n### Role Registry\\n\\nRoles are defined in `.roles/<role-name>/` directories:\\n\\n```\\n.roles/\\n├── developer/\\n│   ├── role.md        # frontmatter + markdown body\\n│   └── skills.md      # optional: skill list\\n├── researcher/\\n│   ├── role.md\\n│   └── skills.md\\n├── tester/\\n│   └── role.md\\n└── ...\\n```\\n\\n**role.md frontmatter format:**\\n\\n```yaml\\n---\\nname: developer\\ndescription: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests\\narchetype: implementer          # implementer | analyst | reviewer | tester\\nstart_status: development       # FIRST child command sets this status after launch\\nend_status: to-review           # role handoff status; for producers this means ready for review, not done\\ndod:                            # definition of done — added to board checklist on spawn\\n  - Code written per task description and AC\\n  - text: Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\\n    classes: [code]\\n  - text: Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\\n    classes: [code]\\nskills:                         # optional: inline skill list (merged with skills.md)\\n  - go-tui-testing-tools\\n---\\n\\n# developer\\n\\n## Description\\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\\n\\n## Standing Orders\\n1. Add or update relevant tests for changed behavior.\\n2. Run the relevant tests before handoff.\\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes.\\n\\n## Deliverable\\nCode + tests.\\n...\\n```\\n\\nEvery board element has a `task_class`: `code` (the default), `metadata`, `docs`, or `research`. Set it with `create(..., task_class=metadata)` or `set_details(ID, task_class=metadata)`, and inspect it through the `task_class` q projection.\\n\\nRole `dod` entries may stay as plain strings, which apply to every task class, or use an object with `text` and `classes`. Spawn injects object entries only when the element's `task_class` appears in `classes`; an omitted or empty `classes` list preserves the legacy all-classes behavior.\\n\\n**skills.md format** (optional, merged with `skills:` in frontmatter):\\n```markdown\\n## Required\\n- project-management\\n- go-tui-testing-tools\\n\\n## Domain\\n- core-data (when working on persistence)\\n- swiftui (when building UI)\\n```\\n\\n**Resolution order** (`LoadRole`):\\n1. Project-local directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`\\n2. Project-local single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`\\n3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`\\n4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`\\n\\nDirectory format takes priority over single file. Project-local takes priority over global.\\n\\n**Global role installation:** `scripts/setup.sh` copies the bundled roles from `.roles/` into `~/.roles/`, making them available in any project without a local `.roles/` directory.\\n\\n**Available roles:**\\n\\n| Role | Archetype | Start → End Status |\\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\\n| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\\n| `developer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `tester` | tester | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\\n| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` → verdict-driven (`done` / `to-dev` / `analysis` / `blocked`) |\\n| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\\n| `coordinator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\\n\\nThe reviewer role intentionally has no unconditional `end_status`. Its role body selects exactly one verdict branch, so the default spawn template cannot overwrite a requested-changes or stop-the-line verdict with `done`. Only an accepted review sets `done`.\\n\\n### How Roles Work with Spawn\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** Spawning without a role produces a crippled agent with no identity, skills, DoD, or lifecycle commands. Model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices and never inherit task-board defaults. `--agent` remains optional: pass a human/task-context provider only when the effective `preferred_agentic_system` allows it; omission uses the exclusive pin, an in-set mixed affinity, legacy preference, or plain affinity as applicable.\\n\\nThe spawn pipeline:\\n1. Loads role config from `.roles/<role>/role.md`\\n2. Loads task metadata (README + precondition resources)\\n3. Computes pre-launch board updates:\\n   - Sets agent name with archetype prefix, e.g. `[implementer] developer (codex)`\\n   - Adds DoD items as checklist entries on the board element\\n4. Assembles prompt from template: role body, skills, DoD, status commands, task metadata\\n5. Persists prompt and spawn-run state under `.temp/prompts/` and `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`\\n6. Returns the `RUN-...` handle immediately\\n7. Background runner claims a queue slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the real child agent, streams logwork, and executes completion hooks\\n8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\\n\\n```bash\\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n```\\n\\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\\n\\nFor coordinator orchestration:\\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\\n\\n### Skill Injection\\n\\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\\n\\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\\n\\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\\n\\nFor sub-agent prompt templates (implementation agent and coordinator review workflow), see [`references/sub-agent-templates.md`](references/sub-agent-templates.md). Its legacy decomposition-agent template must not be used for spec-to-board layout. A `solution-architect` child produces only a genuine architecture decision; the Orchestrator applies that decision to the board inline.\\n\\nKey points covered there:\\n- Reality check: how to maximize sub-agent compliance with task-board updates\\n- Do not use the legacy decomposition-agent template for board layout; delegate only the architecture-decision deliverable that informs later inline board refinement\\n- Implementation agent template (for execution phase)\\n- Coordinator review workflow (approve / return for rework)\\n\\n## Spawning Tracked Background Runs\\n\\n**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.\\n\\nAgent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.\\n\\nUse `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\\n\\n`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.\\n\\n**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:\\n- task description comes from the element README\\n- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element\\n- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\\n- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts\\n\\nHidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.\\n\\nIf the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.\\n\\nThe prompt also includes:\\n1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)\\n2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)\\n3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)\\n4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)\\n\\n**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.\\n\\n**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.\\n`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board\\nlookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.\\n`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the\\nonly opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes\\n`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`\\nonce for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider\\nallowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child\\nlaunches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an\\noptional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a\\nsanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry\\ncomposition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.\\n\\n**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It\\nrequires `agents-infra compose` to implement\\n`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a\\nsuccessful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact\\nCodex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the\\nresponse is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain\\nfully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with\\nsanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.\\n\\nThe private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the\\ndetached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under\\n`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,\\ncommands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve\\nonly MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values\\nremain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply\\nprimary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,\\nprompt, or goal settings from infra composition.\\n`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively\\nexecute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.\\nCanonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider\\nbefore ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set\\nprovider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`\\nand then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference\\nusability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.\\nFor each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the\\nconfigured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any\\n`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured\\nprovider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections\\nuse the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.\\nOptional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent\\nceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw\\nconfigured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits\\nreport the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.\\n\\n**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.\\n\\n**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.\\n\\nFor spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).\\n\\n---\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n18-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,300p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n19-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,300p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: project-management\\ndescription: >\\n  File-based task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration for AI agents.\\n  Use when creating or maintaining boards, decomposing specs into Epic -> Story\\n  -> Task/Bug work, tracking bugs, checking status/progress, or coordinating\\n  execution through task-board. For board-driven execution, the active role of a\\n  normal non-spawned session is ORCHESTRATOR (coordinator/supervisor): plan from\\n  the critical path, create and refine board items, then delegate research,\\n  implementation, testing, docs, and review to tracked background child runs\\n  with explicit role, model, and reasoning selections; child-agent type follows\\n  the current orchestrator runtime unless the human or task context explicitly\\n  overrides it. Before launch,\\n  inspect the project spawn policy: spawn.enabled can disable all child runs and\\n  per-agent model_criterion=equal pins the executable model without model ranking;\\n  ordered criteria use provider-local model ranks and require deliberate\\n  one-line selection rationale plus content-bound acknowledgement after any\\n  requested-to-resolved adjustment.\\n  Spec-to-board decomposition is the Orchestrator's inline duty: never create a\\n  meta-task or spawn solution-architect solely for board layout. Do not perform\\n  task work inline unless the human explicitly asks for inline execution, except\\n  for this mandatory board-layout duty.\\n  Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи,\\n  эпик, стори, таски, скил, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, агенты, критический\\n  путь, декомпозиция, статус, прогресс, гол, голы, цель.\\ntriggers:\\n  - creating task board from spec\\n  - breaking down spec into tasks\\n  - project planning\\n  - task management\\n  - creating bugs\\n  - bug tracking\\n  - epic, story, task, board, task-board\\n  - борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски\\n  - спека, требования, декомпозиция, разбей на задачи\\n  - что дальше, какие задачи, статус, прогресс, покажи борд\\n  - spawn, orchestrator, coordinator, sub-agent, multi-agent\\n  - goal, delivery goal, goal-scope, goal-bound pool\\n  - спавн, оркестратор, координатор, субагент, агенты, критический путь\\n  - гол, голы, цель, взять пул под гол\\n  - work planning, what's next, status, progress, summary\\n---\\n\\n# Project Management Skill\\n\\nFile-backed project management via `task-board` plus tracked child-process orchestration. Board state lives in git or a board-server; executable work is delegated through role-based spawned agents.\\n\\n## Active Role: Orchestrator\\n\\nYour active role is **Orchestrator**. In the `task-board` role registry, `orchestrator` and `coordinator` are the same supervisor identity; `coordinator` is the older implementation name.\\n\\nUnless the prompt explicitly gives you a spawned specialist role, `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, or the human explicitly asks for inline execution, do not act as a worker. Act as the parent Orchestrator.\\n\\nThe Orchestrator does board and orchestration work only:\\n- create/refine epics, stories, tasks, bugs, AC, checklist DoD, dependencies, and resources\\n- inspect `project_config()`, `summary()`, `plan(..., active=true)`, `agents()`, and `spawn` run state\\n- choose the first unblocked critical-path work and spawn the right specialist roles\\n- own each selected item through producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until a reviewer accepts it and the board reaches `done`\\n- summarize child outcomes for routing/status and report status without delegating routine review back to the human\\n\\n### Inline Spec Decomposition\\n\\nSpec decomposition into board elements is the Orchestrator's own inline duty. Read the spec and directly create or refine its epics, stories, tasks/bugs, acceptance criteria, checklist DoD, and dependencies with `task-board`. This board-layout work is an explicit exception to the normal specialist-delegation rule.\\n\\nDo not create a meta-task whose sole purpose is to lay out a spec on the board. Do not spawn `solution-architect` merely to translate a settled spec into stories and tasks. A `solution-architect` spawn is valid only when the work contains a genuine architecture decision; after that decision, the Orchestrator still owns the resulting board layout inline.\\n\\nDelegation examples:\\n\\n- Invalid: create a `decompose-auth-spec` meta-task and spawn `solution-architect` solely to turn the settled auth spec into stories and tasks.\\n- Invalid: spawn `solution-architect` to copy already-settled requirements, acceptance criteria, and dependencies into board elements.\\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to choose between event-driven and request/response integration across modules, then use the decision while laying out the board inline.\\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to define an ambiguous cross-module contract and ownership boundary before refining the affected stories and tasks inline.\\n\\nThis mandate avoids orchestration work that produces no executable scope: the wordstats probe spent two full `sol/max` sessions and 25 minutes on meta-delegated decomposition before the first real task existed.\\n\\nThe Orchestrator does not do specialist task work inline. The board-layout duty above is the explicit exception. Decision rule: if the next step would produce or validate a project artifact — research, code/config changes, tests, documentation, synthesis/decision docs, or a review verdict — prepare a clear board item and run a tracked background specialist:\\n\\n```bash\\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Codex keeps Codex affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Claude keeps Claude affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def456 --role researcher --background --model claude-opus-4-8\\n\\n# Explicit human/task-context override: pass --agent and override affinity\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi789 --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8\\n```\\n\\nUse `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only at real dependency barriers. Parallelize independent work in the same phase; sequence producer -> reviewer -> dependent consumer work.\\n\\n## Delivery Ownership: Close the Loop\\n\\nOnce execution starts, the Orchestrator remains responsible for delivery:\\n\\n1. Monitor the producer until it reaches `to-review` with outcome evidence.\\n2. Spawn and monitor a reviewer child. `to-review` is an immediate routing trigger, never a stopping point or a request for human review.\\n3. If changes are requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, route to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the appropriate producer.\\n4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.\\n5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.\\n6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.\\n\\nNever ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.\\n\\n## Project-State Monitoring\\n\\nUse the observability preset family for the Orchestrator's broad-to-narrow project-state loop. These are field presets on the existing query operations, not new operations:\\n\\n| Level | Query | Top-level projection |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Project | `summary() { observability-project }` | `observability` |\\n| Epic | `list(type=epic) { observability-epic }` or `get(EPIC-...) { observability-epic }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n| Story | `get(STORY-...) { observability-story }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n| Task/Bug | `get(TASK-...) { observability-task }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\\n\\nUse compact output for routine monitoring:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-abc123) { observability-story }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { observability-task }'\\n```\\n\\n`observability-task` is also the leaf preset for Bugs. All four presets expose one stable nested shape:\\n\\n- `observability.scope` is `{type:\\\"project\\\", name:\\\"Project\\\"}` at project level and `{id, type, name}` for an element subtree.\\n- `observability.summary` preserves the existing summary shape, scoped to the project or selected element plus its descendants: `byType` status counts, `active` element rows, and `blocked` rows with `blockedBy`, `derivedBlockedBy`, `isBlocked`, and `isAdvisoryBlocked`.\\n- `observability.readiness.estimateKind` is the leaf or calculated estimate state: `notEstimated`, `estimated`, or `partiallyEstimated` as applicable.\\n- `observability.readiness.coverage` always contains numeric `estimated`, `unestimated`, and `total`, string `ratio` such as `\\\"2/3\\\"`, and boolean `complete`.\\n- `observability.readiness.rolledEstimate` is always the known sum shape `{kind:\\\"estimated\\\", scale:{kind:\\\"fibonacci\\\", number:N}}`, including when coverage is partial.\\n- `observability.readiness.isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard blocking in the scope. `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` separately aggregate derived cross-container waiting. The ID arrays are sorted.\\n\\nPlain `summary()` keeps its legacy `{summary:{byType, active, blocked}}` response. Select `observability-project` when the readiness fields are needed.\\n\\n### Monitoring and routing loop\\n\\n1. **Project readiness:** start with `summary() { observability-project }`. Read `summary.active` for work that needs supervision or review routing, `summary.blocked` for blocker details, and `readiness` for aggregate estimate coverage and hard/advisory state. This is the fastest board-wide routing view.\\n2. **Epic readiness:** list `observability-epic` rows to identify which delivery subtree owns a partial-coverage or blocker signal. Compare the top-level Epic `status` with its scoped `summary.active`; a quiet-looking container can still have active descendants, and a live container can still contain uncovered estimates.\\n3. **Story readiness:** query the affected Story with `observability-story`. Story coverage is over its direct Task/Bug leaves; use the scoped active and blocked rows to decide which producer, reviewer, or blocker should move next.\\n4. **Task/Bug readiness:** query the next leaf with `observability-task`. Coverage `1/1` plus no hard block means its estimate precondition is covered, but launch order still comes from dependencies and `plan(..., active=true)`. Coverage `0/1` or `estimateKind=notEstimated` means a new leaf needs an explicit estimate before entering `development`.\\n\\nWhen any project, Epic, or Story view reports `coverage.complete=false` or `estimateKind=partiallyEstimated`, do not call the rolled estimate a full-scope estimate. Narrow to incomplete Epics/Stories, then request the separate estimate projection:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(EPIC-260218-abc123) { estimate }'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-def456) { estimate }'\\n```\\n\\nThe partial `estimate` projection adds the exact `notEstimatedCount` and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds` to its known `rolledEstimate`. Set or route estimates for those concrete Task/Bug IDs before using the roll-up for capacity or scope decisions. Missing estimates are missing coverage, not zero-point work. Legacy leaves without a persisted Estimate section remain start-compatible, but still appear as unestimated in container projections.\\n\\nRead blocker signals independently:\\n\\n- `isBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is hard-blocked; inspect `blockedElementIds` and the matching `summary.blocked[*].blockedBy`, then advance those manual blockers before launching the dependent leaf.\\n- `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is waiting because of derived cross-container ordering; inspect `advisoryBlockedElementIds` and `derivedBlockedBy`, schedule the source first, but do not treat advisory state as status enforcement.\\n- Both flags may be true in the same scope. Never collapse a mixed signal into one generic blocked label.\\n\\n`coverage.complete` describes estimate coverage only; it is not a delivery-ready or accepted verdict. `rolledEstimate` sums all known scoped leaf estimates regardless of lifecycle status, so it is not remaining work. Combine observability with `plan(..., active=true)` for waves/critical path, `agents()` for assignment state, and `spawn status/events/watch/wait` for child-run health.\\n\\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\\n\\nDo not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \\\"solution\\\" is mostly compensating hacks.\\n\\nThe Bluetooth permission/off-state failure pattern is the canonical warning sign: once the agent learned that iOS could not reveal powered-off Bluetooth state before permission without touching CoreBluetooth and potentially triggering the system prompt, it should have stopped, documented the constraint and options, and asked for a product decision. Adding more booleans, stubs, priority rules, and UI tests only made an invalid model look temporarily testable.\\n\\nPause or escalate only for either an evidence-backed external blocker that cannot be resolved autonomously or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only boundary—an impossible platform/API or product constraint, approval boundary, or unresolved product or architecture tradeoff—that cannot be derived from the task, AC, or existing decisions.\\n\\nWhen this happens:\\n- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.\\n- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.\\n- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.\\n- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.\\n\\nTests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\\nRoutine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Part 1: Task Management\\n\\n---\\n\\n## When to Use\\n\\n- New project with SPEC/requirements → break down into epics/stories/tasks\\n- Need progress tracking in files\\n- Working with AI agent on a project\\n- Tracking bugs\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CLI Tool: `task-board`\\n\\nAll board operations go through the `task-board` CLI. No manual file editing.\\n\\n**Source repo:** your local `skill-project-management` checkout\\n**Installed skill artifact:** `~/.agents/skills/project-management` (degitized copy for agent runtimes)\\n**In PATH:** installed copy in `~/.local/bin/task-board`\\n\\nInstall/update the local binary before board setup:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nFor a safe reinstall that refreshes binaries, the installed skill copy, roles, and install metadata without touching the current project's `task-board.config.json`, use:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/setup.sh --install-only\\n```\\n\\n`--install-only` is also the supported verification gate for rollout drift. It checks the installed `task-board` binary with `project_config()` and local `models()` queries, requires the tracked background, explicit-selection, and child launch-composition contract fields plus current Codex registry to be present, verifies the installed `SKILL.md` matches this source artifact, and fails loud if PATH resolves a stale binary.\\n\\n`task-board self-update` relies on `~/.config/task-board/install.json`, which is written by `scripts/setup.sh`. Do not treat `~/.agents/skills/project-management` as the update source; it is the installed artifact copy and has git metadata scrubbed.\\n\\n`scripts/setup.sh` is the single supported install/update entrypoint for `task-board`, `task-board-tui`, and the installed skill artifact. `make install-*` targets are binary build helpers and do not replace setup.\\n\\nWhen you bootstrap board-driven work inside a real code repository, prefer a single source-checkout setup command that also bootstraps the repo-local agent runtime through `relux-agents-infra`:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\\n```\\n\\n`--bootstrap-local-agents` runs `agents-infra setup local /path/to/project` after writing the project config. Install the global `agents-infra` launcher once from the `relux-agents-infra` source repo via `./setup.sh`, then use this shortcut for project repos.\\n\\nThat local setup makes project `.agents/` the installed runtime copy and refreshes `.claude/` + `.codex/` as thin shims into it. Do this right after board/config setup so spawned agents see the repo-local instructions, skills, and templates instead of drifting onto only global state.\\n\\n### Quick Reference\\n\\nAll reads use `q` (query DSL), all writes use `m` (mutate DSL). Compact format (`--format compact`) saves tokens.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Queries (reads)\\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations, mutations\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # effective runtime + spawn.enabled and per-agent model criteria; run before spawning/audits\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # board overview (counts + active/blocked)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc123) { full }'                 # full element details\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # filtered list\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # blocked tasks\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'  # stories in epic\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'                             # local spawn model/effort registry (also in remote board mode)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=qwen)'                              # Qwen model/effort/context/Token Plan registry\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc123, mode=children)'          # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc123, STORY-260218-def456], mode=children)'  # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'count(type=task, status=done)'                    # count elements\\n\\n# Mutations (writes)\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\\\"...\\\")'                        # create epic\\ntask-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123, name=audio-capture)'            # create story\\ntask-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=impl, description=\\\"...\\\")'  # create task\\ntask-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-abc123, title=\\\"new title\\\", description=\\\"...\\\")'        # update README fields\\ntask-board m 'rename(TASK-260218-abc123, name=better-task-name)'                            # update overview name/slug without changing ID\\ntask-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc123, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'  # estimate Task/Bug work\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'                           # change status\\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc123, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'      # acknowledge an enforced version-control done transition\\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus)'                                # assign agent\\ntask-board m 'unassign(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                                 # remove assignment\\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                      # add dependency\\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                    # remove dependency\\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc123, item=1)'                                       # check checklist item\\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Write tests\\\")'                   # add checklist item\\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Progress update\\\")'                        # append notes\\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\\\"Full replacement\\\", set=true)'             # replace notes\\ntask-board m 'set_parent(TASK-260218-abc123, parent=STORY-260218-def456)'                   # move to new parent\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'  # create resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"revised\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design v2\\\")'  # replace resource\\ntask-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # preview destructive op\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # execute destructive op\\n\\n# Batching (semicolons)\\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus); set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'\\n\\n# Spawn tracked background runs (--role, --background, and --model are REQUIRED; Codex/Qwen also require --reasoning-effort)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8 --budget 5.0\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark --reasoning-effort xhigh\\ntask-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123                                            # inspect tracked run state\\ntask-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123                                              # block on tracked run state after RUN exists\\ntask-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123                                            # replay durable run events (supports --cursor/--follow)\\ntask-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123                                             # follow durable run-event notifications\\n\\n# Auth (always local, never sent to server)\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_4lc6...                                        # store token in Keychain and select active user outside repo config\\ntask-board login admin tb_UJTW... --remote https://board.example.com          # explicit remote bootstrap\\ntask-board logout                                                              # remove selected user's token\\ntask-board whoami                                                              # show remote URL + active user + token status\\ntask-board switch-user admin                                                   # switch active user without touching versioned config\\ntask-board q 'whoami()'                                                        # whoami via query DSL\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                # mode, board source, and effective spawn policy\\ntask-board m 'login(user=sampleuser, token=tb_xxx)'                           # login via mutation DSL\\ntask-board m 'switch_user(user=admin)'                                         # switch via mutation DSL\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n--\n21-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '301,600p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# CLI utilities\\ntask-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"                            # full-text search across board files (local only)\\ntask-board validate                                        # check board structure (local only)\\ntask-board repair-links                                    # list unsupported stored container links (local, read-only)\\ntask-board repair-links STORY-260218-abc123 --confirm      # remove selected suspicious links\\ntask-board tui                                             # interactive terminal dashboard\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Task Board Structure\\n\\n### Hierarchy\\n\\n```\\nEpic → Story → Task/Bug\\n```\\n\\n- **Epic** = large feature / module\\n- **Story** = specific functionality within epic\\n- **Task** = atomic unit of work\\n- **Bug** = defect tracked within a story\\n\\n### Naming Convention\\n\\n**Distributed IDs** — each element gets a unique ID based on date + random hash.\\n\\n```\\nEPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\\n  STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\\n    TASK-260203-g7h8i9_audiorecorder-interface/\\n    TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\\n    BUG-260203-m3n4o5_some-bug/\\n```\\n\\nFormat: `{TYPE}-{YYMMDD}-{hash}_{kebab-case-name}`\\n- `EPIC-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `STORY-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `TASK-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n- `BUG-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\\n\\nWhere:\\n- `YYMMDD` — creation date (year, month, day)\\n- `xxxxxx` — 6-character base36 hash (collision-resistant)\\n\\nIDs are globally unique and work in parallel environments (no counter conflicts).\\n\\n### Directory Layout\\n\\n```\\n.task-board/\\n├── .resources/                   # Flat resource storage (managed by CLI)\\n│   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/       # Resources keyed by element ID\\n│   │   ├── design.md             # Precondition: input for the task\\n│   │   └── results.md            # Outcome: work product\\n│   └── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\\n│       └── research.md\\n├── EPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\\n│   ├── README.md                 # Epic description, scope, AC\\n│   ├── progress.md               # Status, blockedBy, checklist, notes, resource refs\\n│   ├── STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\\n│   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   ├── progress.md\\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_interface/\\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\\n│   │   └── BUG-260203-m3n4o5_crash-on-start/\\n│   │       ├── README.md\\n│   │       └── progress.md\\n│   └── STORY-260203-p6q7r8_amplitude/\\n│       └── ...\\n└── EPIC-260203-s9t0u1_storage/\\n    └── ...\\n```\\n\\nNo `system.md` needed — IDs are self-contained and don't require global counters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Statuses\\n\\n9 statuses: `backlog` → `analysis` → `to-dev` → `development` → `to-review` → `reviewing` → `done`. Plus `closed` and `blocked`.\\n\\nBefore a newly created Task/Bug enters `development`, set its estimate with\\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The start\\ntransition returns a typed estimate-required error while the explicit value is\\n`notEstimated`. Legacy Task/Bug files that have no Estimate section are\\ngrandfathered and remain startable.\\n\\n`done` is accepted leaf work or computed container completion; `closed` is an\\nexplicit administrative disposition. A story or epic may remain `closed` only\\nwhile every direct and transitive descendant is `done` or `closed`.\\n\\nFor full status flow, auto-promotion/reopen, blocked vs is_blocked — see [`references/statuses.md`](references/statuses.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## File Formats\\n\\nEach element directory contains two files:\\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\\n\\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\\n\\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Resources & Bugs\\n\\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create resource\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings\\\")'\\n\\n# Replace existing resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"revised findings\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings v2\\\")'\\n\\n# Delete resource\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\\n```\\n\\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\\n\\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\\n\\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\\n\\n#### Spec\\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\\n\\n#### Plan on the Board\\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\\n\\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\\n\\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\\n\\nKey points:\\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\\n\\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\\n\\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\\n\\nKey points:\\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\\n\\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\\n\\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\\n\\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\", ac=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\\n\\n### Project Start\\n\\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\\n\\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\\n\\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\\n\\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\\n\\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\\n\\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\\n\\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\\n\\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\\n\\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\\n\\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\\n\\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\\nfor the operator contract.\\n\\n| Task type | Role |\\n|-----------|------|\\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\\n\\n**Workflow:**\\n\\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Handle edge case X\\\")'\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Preserve existing API response shape\\\")'\\n   ```\\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\\n   ```bash\\n   # Inline text instruction\\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Implementation guide\\\")'\\n\\n   # File or URL attachment\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\\n\\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\\n   agents-attachments materialize\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \\\"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\\\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\\n   ```\\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n22-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '601,900p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n23:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '601,900p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\nSpawn is task-centric:\\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\\n- Task description comes from the board README\\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \\\"Fused Spawn And Wait\\\"\\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\"`; do not assume live in-session push\\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\\n\\n### Review Policy\\n\\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\\n\\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\\n\\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\\n\\n### Model Selection Guide\\n\\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\\n\\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\\n\\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\\n\\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\\n\\n```bash\\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\\\"\\n\\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\\\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\\n```\\n\\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\\n\\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\\n\\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\\n\\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\\n\\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\\n\\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\\n\\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\\n\\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\\n\\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Implementation notes\\\")'`\\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\\n\\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\", not \\\"done\\\"/\\\"complete\\\"/\\\"finished\\\"/\\\"final\\\"/\\\"готово\\\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\\n\\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\\n\\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\\nordering.\\n\\n```bash\\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\\n\\n# Remove dependency\\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\\n```\\n\\n### Board Review\\n\\n```bash\\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\\n\\n# Full-text search (local only)\\ntask-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"\\n\\n# Validation (local only)\\ntask-board validate\\ntask-board repair-links\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\\n\\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\\n\\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"` (local only)\\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\\n\\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\\n\\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\\n\\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\\n\\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tips\\n\\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \\\"works\\\" is bad, \\\"test passes\\\" is good\\n- **Update progress immediately** — don't defer\\n- **Use checklist** for tracking subtasks within a task\\n- **Bugs live in stories** — always attach to a relevant story\\n- **Sub-agents write tests** — every sub-agent is responsible for tests in its scope\\n- **Supervisor coordinates reviews** — coordinator must spawn/review-route child reviewer work before marking done\\n- **Orchestrator closes the loop** — the parent owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`; it never stops at `to-review` or asks the human to perform routine review\\n- **Rendering is human visualization on explicit request only** — agents never render or open graphs to derive waves or the critical path. The query model already provides that data; see [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) for the centralized render examples\\n- **Verify agents early** — run `task-board q 'agents()'` within 2 minutes of launch; if status not updated, stop and restart with better prompt\\n- **Concrete IDs only** — never use placeholders like `TASK-XXX` in prompts; always use real IDs like `TASK-260203-ae3fcd`\\n- **Human-facing references need ID + title/name** — when mentioning an epic, story, task, or bug in chat, include both so people can understand the reference without looking up the ID\\n- **No work without a task** — every code change must be tracked on the board. If a task doesn't exist, create one first. Don't start coding then retroactively create tasks\\n- **Parent is orchestrator, not worker** — the parent agent plans, creates/refines board items, spawns children, watches `RUN-...` handles, sends directives, and decides next routing. It must not do implementation, research, testing, docs, or review inline when a board task can be delegated.\\n- **Spec decomposition stays inline** — creating/refining the board hierarchy from a settled spec is the Orchestrator's own work. Never create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely for board layout; use that role only when a genuine architecture decision must be made first.\\n- **Do not force-fit broken assumptions** — pause only for a concrete external blocker or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Document the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed; do not build a tower of stubs, flags, priority rules, and tests around an invalid model.\\n- **All specialist deliverable work through tracked background spawn** — every specialist board task MUST run in a separate CLI process with explicit `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also require explicit `--reasoning-effort`. Inline settled-spec board layout is the Orchestrator's explicit exception. Never use an untracked Task helper or implicit model/reasoning selection for specialist deliverables.\\n- **Reviews are child work too** — when work reaches `to-review`, spawn an explicitly selected reviewer child such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`. Route accepted work to `done` or requested changes into rework and another reviewer cycle.\\n- **`to-review` is not done** — producers must report `to-review` as \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\". Coordinators must not tell the human \\\"done\\\"/\\\"complete\\\"/\\\"finished\\\"/\\\"final\\\"/\\\"готово\\\" until a reviewer has accepted the work and the board item is actually `done`.\\n- **Coordinators track `RUN-...`, not shell jobs** — after spawning, hold onto the returned run id and drive orchestration with `task-board spawn status/events/watch/wait`. Do not rely on `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers to track completion\\n- **Drive unblocked tasks to terminal handoff** — if no objective blocker remains, the spawned worker must keep going until it has checked the relevant checklist items, attached outcome evidence, and reached its producer `end_status` (`to-review`) or exactly one reviewer verdict status (`done`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, or evidence-backed `blocked`). If a child exits early or leaves a task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, retry or reroute through a focused child. Use `blocked` only for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision, with the full evidence packet and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.\\n- **Dependent children are sequenced** — if child B consumes or reviews child A's output, the parent must `spawn wait` child A, inspect status/outcome resources, and only then spawn child B. Parallel fan-out is only for independent tasks.\\n- **Explicit spawn selection is REQUIRED** — always pass `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also need a compatible `--reasoning-effort`. The role drives identity/lifecycle, while model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices\\n- **Provider system, then ceilings** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`; `exclusive` pins, `mixed` uses an explicit in-set `--agent` or in-set affinity, and out-of-set providers are refused before side effects. Legacy `preferred_agent` keeps its best-effort ceiling/executable gate. The selected provider's ceiling then resolves model/reasoning.\\n- **`project_config()` first** — before spawn or spawn-contract review, run `task-board q 'project_config()'` so you know whether the current cwd is local or remote, whether `spawn.enabled` permits launch, and which model criterion is in effect\\n- **Resource CRUD is explicit** — `add_resource` / `resource add` create a new named resource and must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` to replace an existing resource; use `delete_resource` / `resource delete --confirm` to remove one\\n- **Inline text resources via DSL** — use `task-board m 'add_resource(...)'` for create and `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` for replacement when the payload is short text that already fits naturally in DSL\\n- **File and URL attachments via CLI** — use `task-board resource add TASK-... ./file.png --type precondition`, `task-board resource update TASK-... ./file.png`, and `task-board resource delete TASK-... NAME --confirm`\\n- **Artifact names must include the task ID** — when attaching outcome artifacts or task-specific instructions, include the owning task ID in the resource name, e.g. `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`\\n- **Chat attachments need the bridge, not the board** — if the file exists only as a chat attachment and has no local path, install [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra), run `agents-attachments materialize`, then pass the resolved path into `task-board resource add`\\n- **Precondition resources = agent instructions** — when spawning via `task-board spawn`, precondition resources are auto-injected into the agent prompt via the template\\n- **Ignore stale `.temp` worktrees during audits** — when reviewing help/docs/source for spawn drift, search canonical source paths with `.temp/**` excluded; historical `.temp/*/skill-project-management-worktree/` copies can contain obsolete spawn files\\n- **Research goes to `.research/` AND resources** — all research results persist in `.research/{YYMMDD}_{topic}.md` (project-level) AND as outcome resources on the task. See [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md)\\n- **Plans go to `.planning/`** — after phase planning, persist them with `task-board plan --save` to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. See [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md)\\n- **Legacy token in config** — if you see `remote.token` in `task-board.config.json`, suggest running `scripts/migrate-credentials.sh --user USER_ID` to move it to the credentials store\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Remote Board Mode\\n\\nThe board can run as a centralized web service instead of local `.task-board/` directory. Multiple agents connect to the same board via HTTP.\\n\\n### Setup\\n\\n```bash\\n# Start board-server\\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management/tools/board-server\\ngo run . --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db --addr :8090\\n\\n# Set environment (token from first-run output)\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8090\\nexport TASK_BOARD_TOKEN=tb_xxxxx\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\n\\n# Create a board\\ncurl -X POST -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $TASK_BOARD_TOKEN\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"id\\\":\\\"my-project\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"My Project\\\"}' \\\\\\n  $TASK_BOARD_REMOTE/admin/boards\\n\\n# Use normally — q and m route to server automatically\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature)'\\n```\\n\\n### How It Works\\n\\n- `task-board q` and `task-board m` detect `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env and route DSL to the server via HTTP\\n- `task-board q 'schema()'` and `task-board q 'project_config()'` are resolved locally so agents can inspect the current repo runtime even when normal reads/writes target a remote board\\n- All mutations (status changes, linking, assignment, auto-promotion) happen server-side\\n- Multiple agents can work on the same board concurrently — bbolt handles serialization\\n- `task-board resource add/update/delete/get` work in both local and remote mode for normal local-path and URL attachments\\n- [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra) is only needed when the file exists only as a chat attachment and must be materialized first\\n- `grep` and `validate` are local-only; `tree` and `task-board plan --render` can present data fetched from either a local or remote board\\n- `spawn` reads board info via remote, but launches agent process locally\\n- `task-board export` / `task-board import` work in both local and remote mode\\n\\n### Config Resolution\\n\\nPriority:\\n- explicit remote: `--remote` flag > `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env\\n- mode selection: `TASK_BOARD_MODE` env > `task-board.config.json` (`mode`) > default `local`\\n- unified config path override: `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG` (otherwise `./task-board.config.json`)\\n- mode=`remote`: use `remote` section (`url`, `board`, `tls_no_verify`, optional `spawn`) — **no token or active user in config file**\\n- mode=`local`: use `local` section (`board_dir`, relative path)\\n- top-level `version_control.confirm` defaults to `false`; when enabled, `desired_commit_time` is required and Bug/Story transitions to `done` require `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This includes automatic Story promotion caused by its last Task/Bug becoming terminal. The acknowledgement decision and the child/ancestor status writes share one serialized mutation boundary in local and remote mode. Before sending an enforcing remote mutation, the CLI requires `GET /v1/version` to advertise `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1`, then verifies the response acknowledgement too. The acknowledgement applies only to the mover's scope; no git clean-tree check is performed.\\n- `project_config().explicit_remote_override` is true only for remote flag/environment overrides; a remote profile from `task-board.config.json` alone reports false.\\n- The CLI rejects a resolved remote context combined with an explicit local selector: `--board-dir`, `TASK_BOARD_DIR`, or `TASK_BOARD_MODE=local`.\\n- CLI and TUI startup parse the sidecar per section and emit non-fatal stderr diagnostics for malformed, unknown, and legacy sections. Every diagnostic names the exact dotted section and expected shape; valid sibling sections still select the board. `--json` uses newline-delimited warning objects on stderr while command data stays on stdout. Policy consumers remain strict, so startup tolerance never authorizes an operation that depends on an invalid section.\\n- Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains operational but is diagnosed with the canonical `preferred_agentic_system` shape. The abandoned top-level `agents` policy, plaintext `remote.token`, unknown keys, and the misspelled `preferred_agent_system` are also reported instead of being silently dropped or migrated.\\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check for agents; it shows configured mode, effective mode after explicit overrides, board source, whoami, `spawn.enabled`, and per-agent model policy for the current cwd\\n- Spawn policy is profile-aware: local mode reads root `spawn`; remote mode reads `remote.spawn`, falling back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. Both support `enabled`, `max_parallel`, canonical `preferred_agentic_system`, optional UTC `schedule`, legacy `preferred_agent`, default-on `launch_composition.enabled`, agent ceilings, and role overrides with identical preflight enforcement. `project_config().spawn.config_key_prefix` names the active key path, `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` reports the currently effective mode/set/provenance, `project_config().spawn.schedule` reports the base system, windows, and active window, and `project_config().spawn.launch_composition.config_key` names the active composition field; `max_parallel` controls only the client's local workdir queue, not remote-board-wide concurrency.\\n\\nToken resolution (in order):\\n1. `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` env\\n2. macOS Keychain (`remote.url + selected user`)\\n3. Error: no token — run `task-board login USER TOKEN` or set `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN`\\n\\nKeychain-first auth model:\\n- project config pins only shared board context: `remote.url`, `remote.board`, `tls_no_verify`\\n- selected user lives in non-versioned local credentials state scoped by `remote.url + remote.board` (and legacy `remote.user` is only a backward-compat fallback)\\n- token lives in Keychain, not in git and not in `task-board.config.json`\\n- `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` is env override/bootstrap only, not the normal storage path\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n24-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '901,1200p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n25-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '901,1200p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"- `task-board login/logout/switch-user/whoami` manage the selected-user flow\\n\\nGlobal non-versioned auth state file:\\n- path: `~/.config/task-board/credentials.json`\\n- shape:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"remotes\\\": {\\n    \\\"https://board.example.com\\\": {\\n      \\\"active_user\\\": \\\"admin\\\",\\n      \\\"active_users\\\": {\\n        \\\"my-project\\\": \\\"alexis\\\",\\n        \\\"ops-project\\\": \\\"admin\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"users\\\": {}\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n- `remotes.<url>.active_users.<board-id>` = selected user for a concrete board on that remote\\n- `remotes.<url>.active_user` = remote-wide fallback before a board is pinned or when no board-specific selection exists\\n- `remotes.<url>.users` = legacy metadata container; in steady state tokens should be in Keychain, not here\\n\\nMigration from legacy plaintext credentials:\\n```bash\\ntask-board migrate-keychain --print-shell-cleanup\\ntask-board migrate-keychain --cleanup-legacy-file\\n```\\n\\nOther:\\n- sidecar default location: project directory (current working directory / `--project-dir` for setup scripts), not global/bin directory\\n- setup/install write unified config into project directory (default: current dir)\\n- CLI startup auto-bootstrap: if local board exists and config file does not exist, CLI writes default local profile (`mode=local`, `local.board_dir=.task-board`)\\n\\n### Board Migration Workflows\\n\\nArchive migration is now the canonical way to move a board between local and remote runtimes.\\n\\nWhat is preserved:\\n- hierarchy (`epic -> story -> task/bug`)\\n- statuses\\n- assignee\\n- original element update timestamps\\n- notes/checklists/dependencies\\n- precondition/outcome resource refs\\n- resource payload files byte-for-byte\\n\\nEach export carries a versioned transfer manifest. Import validates the full\\narchive before mutation, rejects unsafe/duplicate paths and target ID\\ncollisions, and rolls back all writes if applying the archive fails. Remote\\nimports are compressed-size bounded by the server (64 MiB by default).\\n\\nCore commands:\\n```bash\\n# Export current board to archive\\ntask-board export /tmp/board.tar.gz\\n\\n# Import archive into current board\\ntask-board import /tmp/board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Local -> Remote\\n\\n```bash\\n# source project (local mode)\\ncd ~/src/source-project\\ntask-board export /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# remote-connected project\\ncd ~/src/target-project\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Remote -> Local\\n\\n```bash\\n# remote-connected project\\ncd ~/src/source-remote-project\\ntask-board export /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# target local project\\ncd ~/src/target-local-project\\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"local\\\",\\n  \\\"local\\\": {\\n    \\\"board_dir\\\": \\\".task-board\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"spawn\\\": {\\n    \\\"max_parallel\\\": 20\\n  }\\n}\\nJSON\\n\\ntask-board import /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Round-trip Before Deploy\\n\\nUse a locally deployed `board-server` to prove `local -> remote -> local` before touching the real remote service:\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Build a fresh board-server binary for local service validation\\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management\\nmake install-backend\\n\\n# 2. Start local board-server\\nboard-server --addr 127.0.0.1:8091 --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db\\n\\n# 3. Project-local remote config\\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"remote\\\",\\n  \\\"remote\\\": {\\n    \\\"url\\\": \\\"http://127.0.0.1:8091\\\",\\n    \\\"board\\\": \\\"roundtrip-playground\\\",\\n    \\\"tls_no_verify\\\": false,\\n    \\\"spawn\\\": {\\n      \\\"max_parallel\\\": 20\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\nJSON\\n\\n# 4. Store token, create board, import archive\\ntask-board login admin tb_xxxxx\\ntask-board admin board-create roundtrip-playground \\\"Roundtrip Playground\\\"\\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# 5. Export back out and import into a fresh local board\\ntask-board export /tmp/roundtrip.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\nFor local-deployed service mode, if MinIO is not configured, `board-server` automatically uses a filesystem resource backend under `<dataDir>/.resources/`. That is the easiest way to validate resource payload round-trip during development.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Setup & Deployment\\n\\nTwo deployment modes. Choose based on your use case:\\n\\n| | Local | Service |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Board storage** | `.task-board/` directory in project | board-server (bbolt + MinIO) |\\n| **Multi-agent** | Single agent at a time | Multiple agents concurrently |\\n| **Persistence** | Git (committed with project) | Server-side (backed up separately) |\\n| **Network** | None | HTTP (LAN or remote) |\\n| **Setup time** | Zero — just use CLI | 2 min (make) or 5 min (Docker) |\\n| **Best for** | Solo dev, small projects | Teams, swarm agents, persistent boards |\\n\\n### Local mode (default)\\n\\nNo board bootstrap needed. CLI creates `.task-board/` on first `create` command.\\nIf you run setup/install scripts, profile configs are created automatically:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Canonical workstation setup for CLI/TUI + installed skill artifact\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode local --board-dir .task-board\\n\\n# Start using\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\\\"...\\\")'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\n```\\n\\nBoard lives in `.task-board/` inside your project. Commit it to git for persistence.\\n\\nIf this board lives inside a real code repository that agents will work on, bootstrap it from the `skill-project-management` source checkout with repo-local agents enabled:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\\n```\\n\\nThis is the expected companion step for board setup in a project repo:\\n- `.agents/` gets the local runtime contents and templates\\n- `.claude/` and `.codex/` are refreshed as thin links into `.agents/`\\n- spawned agents can reliably see repo-local instructions instead of only global installs\\n- agents can call `task-board q 'project_config()'` before work to verify whether this repo uses a local board or a remote profile\\n\\n### Service mode (board-server)\\n\\n#### Quick start (local binary)\\n\\n```bash\\n# Install CLI/TUI + project profile via the canonical setup path\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\\n\\n# If this board drives work in a code repo, prefer the single setup command with repo-local agents\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-board my-project --bootstrap-local-agents\\n\\n# Build board-server binary (binary-only helper)\\nmake install-backend\\n\\n# Start server locally\\nboard-server --addr :8080 --data-dir ./data/boards --auth-db ./data/auth.db\\n```\\n\\nServer prints everything you need on startup:\\n```\\n  board-server running:\\n  URL:         http://localhost:8080\\n  Data:        ./data/boards\\n\\n  FIRST RUN — admin token (save it, shown once):\\n  tb_xxxxx\\n\\n  Quick start:\\n    export TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\n    export TASK_BOARD_ID=<board-id>\\n```\\n\\nSelect a user, store the token in Keychain, then go:\\n```bash\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\\n\\n# Create a board\\ntask-board admin board create my-project \\\"My Project\\\"\\n\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\\n\\n# Use normally — all DSL commands route to server\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system)'\\n\\n# Optional: write remote profile config explicitly\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\\n```\\n\\n#### Docker deployment (production)\\n\\nFull stack: Caddy (TLS reverse proxy) + board-server + MinIO (resource storage).\\n\\n```bash\\ncd tools/board-server/deploy\\ncp .env.example .env\\n# Edit .env: set BOARD_DOMAIN, MinIO credentials\\n\\n./deploy.sh         # Build + start (prints admin token on first run)\\n./status.sh         # Check health\\n./logs.sh           # Tail logs\\n./backup.sh         # Backup data\\n./stop.sh           # Stop all\\n./e2e-test.sh       # End-to-end test\\n```\\n\\nFor self-signed TLS (localhost/LAN):\\n```bash\\n# CLI flag\\ntask-board --insecure q --format compact 'summary()'\\n\\n# Or environment (inherited by spawned agents)\\nexport TASK_BOARD_TLS_NO_VERIFY=1\\n\\n# Or unified config file (project-local)\\n# ./task-board.config.json  (NOTE: no token here — use 'task-board login')\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\":\\\"remote\\\",\\n  \\\"local\\\":{\\\"board_dir\\\":\\\".task-board\\\"},\\n  \\\"remote\\\":{\\n    \\\"url\\\":\\\"https://board.local\\\",\\n    \\\"board\\\":\\\"my-project\\\",\\n    \\\"tls_no_verify\\\":true,\\n    \\\"spawn\\\":{\\\"enabled\\\":true,\\\"max_parallel\\\":20}\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n#### User management (service mode)\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create user + token\\ntask-board admin user create alice\\ntask-board admin token create alice\\n\\n# Grant board access\\ntask-board admin access grant alice my-project editor\\n\\n# List users\\ntask-board admin user list\\n```\\n\\nRoles: `admin` (full access), `editor` (read/write board), `viewer` (read-only).\\n\\n### Makefile reference\\n\\nThese targets are build helpers. They are not a replacement for `./scripts/setup.sh` when you need the installed skill copy, role sync, or self-update metadata.\\n\\n```bash\\nmake                    # install-frontend (CLI + TUI)\\nmake install-frontend   # CLI + TUI — developer workstation\\nmake install-backend    # CLI + server — deployment\\nmake install            # all three binaries\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n26-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1201,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n27:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1201,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"make test               # run all tests\\nmake snapshot           # goreleaser snapshot (cross-platform build)\\nmake clean              # remove binaries + dist/\\n```\\n\\n---\\n---\\n\\n# Part 2: Planning\\n\\nPlan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).\\n\\nCanonical planning API:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees\\n```\\n\\nScope forms:\\n\\n- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)\\n- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)\\n- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.\\n\\nCompact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:\\n\\n```\\nactive:false\\ncriticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04\\nelements:4\\nmode:children\\nscope:EPIC-01\\nstatuses:{\\\"TASK-01\\\":\\\"done\\\",\\\"TASK-02\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"TASK-03\\\":\\\"backlog\\\",\\\"TASK-04\\\":\\\"backlog\\\"}\\nwaves:[[\\\"TASK-01\\\",\\\"TASK-03\\\"],[\\\"TASK-02\\\"],[\\\"TASK-04\\\"]]\\n```\\n\\n- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel\\n- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain\\n- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)\\n\\nThe default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.\\n\\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board plan                                    # full project plan\\ntask-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan\\ntask-board plan --save                             # save to file\\n```\\n\\nAgents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Part 3: Agent Tracking\\n\\n## Assigning Agents\\n\\n```bash\\n# Assign agent to element\\ntask-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1)'\\n\\n# Remove assignment\\ntask-board m 'unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'\\n```\\n\\n## Agent Dashboard\\n\\n```bash\\n# Show active agents\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'\\n\\n# Custom stale threshold (minutes)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\\n```\\n\\nDashboard response includes each agent's name, assigned element list (`id`, `name`, `status`), and `totalAssigned`, plus top-level `totalAgents`. The `stale` parameter filters old `done` items; it does not return timestamps or a child-progress counter.\\n\\n## Sub-Agent Workflow\\n\\n1. Coordinator breaks work into stories, assigns agents:\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1); assign(STORY-260218-def, agent=agent-2)'\\n   ```\\n2. Each sub-agent works on its scope, updating task statuses\\n3. Coordinator monitors: `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n4. When all done — agents disappear from dashboard\\n\\n## Agent Roles\\n\\nRoles define what an agent does, which skills it needs, what statuses it drives, and its definition of done.\\n\\n### Role Registry\\n\\nRoles are defined in `.roles/<role-name>/` directories:\\n\\n```\\n.roles/\\n├── developer/\\n│   ├── role.md        # frontmatter + markdown body\\n│   └── skills.md      # optional: skill list\\n├── researcher/\\n│   ├── role.md\\n│   └── skills.md\\n├── tester/\\n│   └── role.md\\n└── ...\\n```\\n\\n**role.md frontmatter format:**\\n\\n```yaml\\n---\\nname: developer\\ndescription: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests\\narchetype: implementer          # implementer | analyst | reviewer | tester\\nstart_status: development       # FIRST child command sets this status after launch\\nend_status: to-review           # role handoff status; for producers this means ready for review, not done\\ndod:                            # definition of done — added to board checklist on spawn\\n  - Code written per task description and AC\\n  - text: Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\\n    classes: [code]\\n  - text: Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\\n    classes: [code]\\nskills:                         # optional: inline skill list (merged with skills.md)\\n  - go-tui-testing-tools\\n---\\n\\n# developer\\n\\n## Description\\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\\n\\n## Standing Orders\\n1. Add or update relevant tests for changed behavior.\\n2. Run the relevant tests before handoff.\\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes.\\n\\n## Deliverable\\nCode + tests.\\n...\\n```\\n\\nEvery board element has a `task_class`: `code` (the default), `metadata`, `docs`, or `research`. Set it with `create(..., task_class=metadata)` or `set_details(ID, task_class=metadata)`, and inspect it through the `task_class` q projection.\\n\\nRole `dod` entries may stay as plain strings, which apply to every task class, or use an object with `text` and `classes`. Spawn injects object entries only when the element's `task_class` appears in `classes`; an omitted or empty `classes` list preserves the legacy all-classes behavior.\\n\\n**skills.md format** (optional, merged with `skills:` in frontmatter):\\n```markdown\\n## Required\\n- project-management\\n- go-tui-testing-tools\\n\\n## Domain\\n- core-data (when working on persistence)\\n- swiftui (when building UI)\\n```\\n\\n**Resolution order** (`LoadRole`):\\n1. Project-local directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`\\n2. Project-local single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`\\n3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`\\n4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`\\n\\nDirectory format takes priority over single file. Project-local takes priority over global.\\n\\n**Global role installation:** `scripts/setup.sh` copies the bundled roles from `.roles/` into `~/.roles/`, making them available in any project without a local `.roles/` directory.\\n\\n**Available roles:**\\n\\n| Role | Archetype | Start → End Status |\\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\\n| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\\n| `developer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `tester` | tester | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\\n| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` → verdict-driven (`done` / `to-dev` / `analysis` / `blocked`) |\\n| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\\n| `coordinator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\\n\\nThe reviewer role intentionally has no unconditional `end_status`. Its role body selects exactly one verdict branch, so the default spawn template cannot overwrite a requested-changes or stop-the-line verdict with `done`. Only an accepted review sets `done`.\\n\\n### How Roles Work with Spawn\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** Spawning without a role produces a crippled agent with no identity, skills, DoD, or lifecycle commands. Model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices and never inherit task-board defaults. `--agent` remains optional: pass a human/task-context provider only when the effective `preferred_agentic_system` allows it; omission uses the exclusive pin, an in-set mixed affinity, legacy preference, or plain affinity as applicable.\\n\\nThe spawn pipeline:\\n1. Loads role config from `.roles/<role>/role.md`\\n2. Loads task metadata (README + precondition resources)\\n3. Computes pre-launch board updates:\\n   - Sets agent name with archetype prefix, e.g. `[implementer] developer (codex)`\\n   - Adds DoD items as checklist entries on the board element\\n4. Assembles prompt from template: role body, skills, DoD, status commands, task metadata\\n5. Persists prompt and spawn-run state under `.temp/prompts/` and `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`\\n6. Returns the `RUN-...` handle immediately\\n7. Background runner claims a queue slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the real child agent, streams logwork, and executes completion hooks\\n8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\\n\\n```bash\\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n```\\n\\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\\n\\nFor coordinator orchestration:\\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\\n\\n### Skill Injection\\n\\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\\n\\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\\n\\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\\n\\nFor sub-agent prompt templates (implementation agent and coordinator review workflow), see [`references/sub-agent-templates.md`](references/sub-agent-templates.md). Its legacy decomposition-agent template must not be used for spec-to-board layout. A `solution-architect` child produces only a genuine architecture decision; the Orchestrator applies that decision to the board inline.\\n\\nKey points covered there:\\n- Reality check: how to maximize sub-agent compliance with task-board updates\\n- Do not use the legacy decomposition-agent template for board layout; delegate only the architecture-decision deliverable that informs later inline board refinement\\n- Implementation agent template (for execution phase)\\n- Coordinator review workflow (approve / return for rework)\\n\\n## Spawning Tracked Background Runs\\n\\n**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.\\n\\nAgent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.\\n\\nUse `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\\n\\n`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.\\n\\n**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:\\n- task description comes from the element README\\n- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element\\n- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\\n- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts\\n\\nHidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.\\n\\nIf the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.\\n\\nThe prompt also includes:\\n1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)\\n2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)\\n3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)\\n4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)\\n\\n**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.\\n\\n**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.\\n`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board\\nlookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.\\n`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the\\nonly opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes\\n`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`\\nonce for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider\\nallowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child\\nlaunches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an\\noptional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a\\nsanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry\\ncomposition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.\\n\\n**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It\\nrequires `agents-infra compose` to implement\\n`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a\\nsuccessful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact\\nCodex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the\\nresponse is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain\\nfully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with\\nsanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.\\n\\nThe private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the\\ndetached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under\\n`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,\\ncommands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve\\nonly MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values\\nremain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply\\nprimary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,\\nprompt, or goal settings from infra composition.\\n`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively\\nexecute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.\\nCanonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider\\nbefore ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set\\nprovider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`\\nand then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference\\nusability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.\\nFor each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the\\nconfigured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any\\n`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured\\nprovider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections\\nuse the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.\\nOptional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent\\nceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw\\nconfigured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits\\nreport the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.\\n\\n**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.\\n\\n**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.\\n\\nFor spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).\\n\\n---\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n28-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,453p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n29-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,453p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: go-testing-tools\\ndescription: >\\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\\ntriggers:\\n  - go test\\n  - bubbletea test\\n  - tuitestkit\\n  - go test helpers\\n  - reducer test\\n  - snapshot test go\\n  - golden file go\\n  - тесты го\\n  - го тест хелперы\\n  - тесты баблти\\n  - тест утилиты го\\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\\n---\\n\\n# Go Testing Tools\\n\\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\\n\\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Prerequisites\\n\\n- **Go 1.21+**\\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\\n\\nInstall the library in your project:\\n\\n```bash\\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\\n\\n```go\\npackage myscreen_test\\n\\nimport (\\n    \\\"testing\\\"\\n\\n    \\\"github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\\\"\\n)\\n\\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\\n\\n    // Press down twice\\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(\\\"down\\\"), tuitestkit.Key(\\\"down\\\"))\\n\\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, \\\"third item\\\")\\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, \\\"ERROR\\\")\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Testing Pyramid\\n\\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\\n\\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\\n\\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 > Level 2 > Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\\n\\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Library API Reference\\n\\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\\n\\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\\n\\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\\n\\n```go\\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\\n// Supports: \\\"enter\\\", \\\"tab\\\", \\\"esc\\\", \\\"space\\\", \\\"backspace\\\", \\\"up\\\", \\\"down\\\",\\n// \\\"left\\\", \\\"right\\\", \\\"home\\\", \\\"end\\\", \\\"pgup\\\", \\\"pgdown\\\", \\\"delete\\\", \\\"insert\\\",\\n// \\\"f1\\\"-\\\"f20\\\", \\\"ctrl+c\\\", \\\"ctrl+a\\\"...\\\"ctrl+z\\\", \\\"alt+h\\\", \\\"alt+enter\\\",\\n// single runes: \\\"a\\\", \\\"/\\\", \\\"1\\\", etc.\\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\\n\\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\\n\\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\\n\\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\\n\\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\\n\\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\\n\\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\\n```\\n\\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\\n\\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\\n\\n```go\\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\\n// Init() is NOT called.\\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\\n\\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\\n\\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\\n```\\n\\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\\n\\n```go\\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(\\\"r\\\"))\\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\\n```\\n\\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\\n\\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\\n\\n```go\\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\\n// S = state type, A = action type.\\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\\n    Name    string\\n    Initial S\\n    Action  A\\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\\n}\\n\\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\\n    Name   string\\n    Action A\\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\\n}\\n\\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\\n    Name    string\\n    Initial S\\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\\n}\\n\\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\\n\\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\\n```\\n\\n**Invariant checking:**\\n\\n```go\\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\\n    Name  string\\n    Check func(s S) error\\n}\\n\\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\\n\\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\\n\\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\\n\\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\\n```\\n\\n**Example:**\\n\\n```go\\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\\n        Name: \\\"cursor in bounds\\\",\\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\\n            if s.Cursor < 0 || s.Cursor >= len(s.Items) {\\n                return fmt.Errorf(\\\"cursor %d out of [0, %d)\\\", s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\\n            }\\n            return nil\\n        },\\n    },\\n)\\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\\n```\\n\\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\\n\\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\\n\\n```go\\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\\ntype MockCall struct {\\n    Method string\\n    Args   []any\\n}\\n\\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\\n\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\\n\\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\\n\\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\\n\\n// Assertion helpers\\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\\n```\\n\\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\\n\\n```go\\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\\n}\\n\\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\\n    return &TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\\n}\\n\\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\\n    m.Record(\\\"TreeJSON\\\")\\n    return m.Responses.Get(\\\"TreeJSON\\\")\\n}\\n\\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\\n    m.Record(\\\"Execute\\\", cmd, args)\\n    return m.Responses.Get(\\\"Execute:\\\" + cmd)\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\\n\\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\\n\\n```go\\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\\n\\n// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\\n\\nfunc ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\\nfunc ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\\nfunc ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\\nfunc ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\\nfunc ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\\nfunc ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\\n\\n// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\\n\\nfunc ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\\nfunc NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\\nfunc LinesFromStr(view string) []string\\nfunc MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\\n```\\n\\n### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\\n\\nGolden file comparison for visual regression testing.\\n\\n```go\\n// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\\n// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\\nvar UpdateSnapshots bool\\n\\n// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\\nfunc SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\\n\\n// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\\nfunc SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\\n\\n// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\\nfunc SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\\n\\n// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\\nfunc SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\\n```\\n\\nGolden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/<name>.golden` relative to the test file.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create or update golden files\\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\\n\\n# Update for a single package\\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\\n```\\n\\nMismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Closed-Loop Workflow\\n\\nThe agent development cycle for writing tests:\\n\\n```\\n1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\\n2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\\n3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\\n4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\\n5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\\n6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\\n7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\\n8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\\n9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\\n```\\n\\n### Step Details\\n\\n**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\\n- What state does it manage?\\n- What messages does it handle in Update?\\n- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\\n- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\\n- What does View() render?\\n\\n**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\\n- Pure state logic -> Level 1 (reducer tests)\\n- Key bindings, view rendering -> Level 2 (component tests)\\n- Data loading, command execution -> Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\\n- Visual appearance -> Level 4 (snapshot tests)\\n\\n**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\\n1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\\n2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\\n3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\\n4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\\n\\n**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\\n- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\\n- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\\n- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\\n- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\\n\\n**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\\n\\n**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\\n\\n**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\\n- Type assertion panic -> `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\\n- View assertion failure -> check ANSI stripping, check exact text\\n- Snapshot mismatch -> run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\\n- Mock assertion failure -> check response key matches (`\\\"Execute:list\\\"` not `\\\"Execute\\\"`)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Templates\\n\\nScaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\\n\\n| Template | Purpose |\\n|----------|---------|\\n| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\\n| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\\n| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\\n| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\\n| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\\n| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\\n\\nCopy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## References\\n\\nIn-depth documentation in `references/`:\\n\\n| Document | Content |\\n|----------|---------|\\n| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\\n| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\\n| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Setup\\n\\nRun the setup script to create symlinks:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nThis links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\\n\\nTo remove:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/deinit.sh\\n```\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n--\n34-{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files -g '\\\"'!vendor'\\\"' -g '\\\"'!.git'\\\"' | sed -n '1,320p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"CONTRIBUTING.md\\nREADME.md\\nNOTICE\\ndocs/implementation-plan.md\\ngo.sum\\nLICENSE\\ngo.mod\\ntask-board.config.json\\ninstall.sh\\nMakefile\\ncmd/curator/main_test.go\\ncmd/curator/main.go\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/assets/templates/snapshot_test.go.tmpl\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/assets/templates/component_test.go.tmpl\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/assets/templates/reducer_test.go.tmpl\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/assets/templates/PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/SPEC.md\\nagents/skills/skill-go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-2g7avf_independent-consumers.svg\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/STORY-260720-35dck7_install-lifecycle.svg\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-1pvfj5_candidate-release-ci-gates.svg\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-1pvfj5_candidate-release-ci-gates.png\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/STORY-260720-35dck7_install-lifecycle.png\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-2g7avf_independent-consumers.png\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/STORY-260720-3plyvy_component-map.svg\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-z2z795_csk-build-lifecycle.png\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/STORY-260720-3plyvy_install-lifecycle.png\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-1zntv0_go-v1-execution-boundary.png\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-z9j4c9_csk-build-components.png\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-3ag6pi_verification-gates.svg\\ndiagrams/artefacts/plantuml/TASK-260720-3pvihp_release-evidence-gates.svg\\